window.QUIZ={"course":{"code":"CS313","key":"cs313.quiz.v1","prof":"★ prof./tutorial question","sub":"A–D, Yes/No and True/False · one chapter per lecture · progress saved in this browser","exams":[{"name":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","when":"Mon 26.10.2026 · 11:00 sharp","scope":"In-class quiz 1 · DFAs, NFAs, NFA → DFA conversion"},{"name":"Tutorial Quiz 1","date":"2026-11-10","when":"Tue 10.11.2026 · 10:00, 45 min, in the tutorial","scope":"In-Tutorial Assignment 1 · 2 questions, one each: (1) regular languages / regular expressions, (2) pumping lemma"},{"name":"Midterm","date":"2026-11-16","when":"Week 8 (date TBA)","scope":"5 parts × 20%: sets · proofs (direct, constructive, contradiction only) · DFA/NFA · regex · pumping lemma"}]},"built":"2026-11-09","chapters":[{"id":"dfa","title":"Lecture 3 · DFAs (Tutorial 3 stands in)","short":"L3 DFA","exam":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","w":1.2,"topics":["Strings & Σ∗","DFA 5-tuple & δ","Tracing & δ∗","Language of a DFA","Completeness & dead state","Regular operations","Closure & product construction","Language algebra"],"n":64},{"id":"nfa","title":"Lecture 4 · NFAs & NFA → DFA","short":"L4 NFA","exam":"Quiz 1","date":"2026-10-26","w":1.2,"topics":["DFA or NFA?","NFA 5-tuple","NFA computation","ε-closure","Subset construction","Pruning & the ∅ state","Equivalence & errata"],"n":65},{"id":"rex","title":"Lecture 5 · Regular expressions & closure","short":"L5 Regex","exam":"Tutorial Quiz 1","date":"2026-11-10","w":1.3,"topics":["Regular operations","Closure constructions","Writing a regex","Describing a regex","Identities & simplifying","True or false","Automata ↔ regex"],"n":35},{"id":"pump","title":"Pumping lemma (M&S §2.9)","short":"Pumping","exam":"Tutorial Quiz 1","date":"2026-11-10","w":1.3,"topics":["Statement","Who chooses what","Choosing s and i","Regular or not?"],"n":29},{"id":"mrev","title":"★ Midterm review (all 5 parts)","short":"Mid review","exam":"Midterm","date":"2026-11-16","w":1.3,"topics":["Sets","Proof techniques","DFA & NFA","Regular expressions","Pumping lemma"],"n":24},{"id":"l01","title":"Lecture 1 · Sets & Operations","short":"L1 Sets","exam":"Midterm","date":"2026-11-16","w":0.6,"topics":["Why theory","Membership","Subsets & equality","Set operations","Power set","Cartesian products","Partitions & big unions"],"n":34},{"id":"l02","title":"Lecture 2 · Relations & Functions","short":"L2 Relations","exam":"Midterm","date":"2026-11-16","w":0.6,"topics":["Relations as sets","Domain & range","Relation properties","Equivalence relations","Cardinality & countability","Functions","Injective & surjective"],"n":38},{"id":"l03","title":"Lecture 2 · Proof Techniques","short":"L2 Proofs","exam":"Midterm","date":"2026-11-16","w":0.6,"topics":["Theorems & proofs","Direct & deductive proofs","Constructive vs nonconstructive","Pigeonhole principle","Proof by contradiction","Induction","Set laws & De Morgan"],"n":30}],"questions":[{"id":"dfa-001","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"mcq","q":"Let Σ = {0, 1, 2}. How many strings are in Σ³ (all strings of length exactly 3)?","options":["9","27","6","81"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"|Σⁿ| = |Σ|ⁿ = 3³ = 27: each of the 3 positions holds any of 3 symbols. 9 = 3·3 multiplies length by size, and 81 = 3⁴ is one length too many.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 1.1(a)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-002","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"mcq","q":"Let Σ = {0, 1, 2}. How many strings over Σ have length at most 2?","options":["12","9","13","27"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"3⁰ + 3¹ + 3² = 1 + 3 + 9 = 13. The 1 is ε, the length-0 string; forgetting it gives the tempting 12.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 1.1(b)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-003","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"tf","q":"Σ∗ contains strings of infinite length, which is why Σ∗ is an infinite set.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"Every string is a finite sequence. Σ∗ is infinite because it has strings of every finite length, not because some string is infinitely long.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 1.1(c)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-004","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"mcq","q":"Which statement is TRUE?","options":["ε ∈ ∅","{ε} = ∅","ε ∈ L for every language L","∅ ⊆ {a, b}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"∅ is a subset of every set. ∅ has no elements (so ε ∉ ∅), {ε} has one element so it is not ∅, and ε ∉ {a} shows a language need not contain ε.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 1.2","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-005","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"mcq","q":"Give the first twelve strings, in lexicographic enumeration, of\nL = {w ∈ {1,2,3}∗ : |w| is even}.","options":["ε, 11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23, 31, 32, 33, 1111, 1112","11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23, 31, 32, 33, 1111, 1112, 1113","ε, 11, 1111, 111111, 11111111, 1111111111, 111111111111, 11111111111111, …","ε, 1, 2, 3, 11, 12, 13, 21, 22, 23, 31, 32"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Lexicographic enumeration in this course = shortlex: shortest first, dictionary order within a length, and ε first because |ε| = 0 is even. Pure dictionary order never gets past 11, 1111, …; odd-length strings like 1 are not in L.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q6 · Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf)","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q6 · First twelve strings of L = {w ∈ {1,2,3}∗ : |w| even}","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-006","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"mcq","q":"First six strings, in shortlex order, of\nL = {w ∈ {a,b}∗ : every a is immediately followed by a b}.","options":["b, ab, bb, abb, bab, bbb","ε, b, ab, ba, bb, abb","ε, ab, b, bb, abb, bab","ε, b, ab, bb, abb, bab"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"ε qualifies (it has no a at all), then b; ab and bb have length 2; then abb, bab. ba is out (its a is last, nothing follows it), and ab before b breaks shortlex (shorter first).","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) (enumeration question)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-007","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"mcq","q":"First six strings, in shortlex order, of L = {w ∈ {a,b}∗ : #a(w) = #b(w)}.","options":["ε, ab, ba, aabb, abab, abba","ab, ba, aabb, abab, abba, baab","ε, ab, ba, abab, abba, aabb","ε, ab, ba, aabb, abab, baba"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Odd lengths never qualify; ε comes first; length 4 in dictionary order is aabb, abab, abba, baab, …. Dropping ε or listing abab before aabb are the usual slips; baba is 6th only after abba and baab.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 1.3","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-008","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"mcq","q":"L = {Austin, Houston, Dallas} (a language of three strings over the Latin letters). How big is L∗?","options":["Finite: 3 strings","Finite: 2³ = 8 strings","Countably infinite","Uncountably infinite"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"L∗ contains ε, Austin, AustinAustin, … so it is infinite; it is a set of finite strings over a finite alphabet, so it can be listed in shortlex order, i.e. it is countable.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) (first question)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-009","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Strings & Σ∗","type":"mcq","q":"In the shortlex enumeration of {a, b}∗, which string comes immediately after bb?","options":["ba","aaa","bba","bbb"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"All length-2 strings end with bb, so the next string is the first of length 3, aaa. bba and bbb would be next only in pure dictionary order, which does not list by length.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §1.3 (shortlex: ε, a, b, aa, ab, ba, bb, aaa, …)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-010","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"mcq","q":"In the 5-tuple M = (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F) of a DFA, what is the type of the transition function?","options":["δ : Q × Σ → 𝒫(Q)","δ : Q × Σ → Q","δ : Q × Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q)","δ : Q × Σ∗ → Q"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"A DFA maps one (state, symbol) pair to exactly one state. Sets of states (𝒫(Q)) and ε inputs belong to NFAs; Q × Σ∗ → Q is the extended function δ∗, built from δ.","source":"M&S Definition 2.2.1 · Sipser Definition 1.5","study":"book/B2-finite-automata-regular.md — 2.2 Deterministic finite automata (DFA)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-011","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"mcq","q":"Let Q = {p, q} and Σ = {a, b}. How many different transition functions δ : Q × Σ → Q are there?","options":["16","4","8","64"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The table has |Q|·|Σ| = 4 cells and each holds one of 2 states: 2⁴ = 16. In general |Q|^(|Q|·|Σ|). 4 is the number of cells, not functions.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.3(b)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-012","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"mcq","q":"A DFA has 3 states over the alphabet {0, 1}. How many different transition functions δ are possible?","options":["216","64","9","729"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"|Q|^(|Q|·|Σ|) = 3^(3·2) = 3⁶ = 729. 216 = 6³ swaps base and exponent; 64 = 2⁶ uses |Σ| as the base.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.3(c) formula","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-013","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"mcq","q":"A DFA has 4 states and alphabet {a, b, c}. How many cells does its transition table have (none may be empty)?","options":["7","64","12","81"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"One row per state, one column per symbol: |Q|·|Σ| = 4·3 = 12 cells, each holding exactly one state. 64 and 81 are counts of functions-style powers, not cells.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.3(a)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-014","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"yn","q":"Q = {p, q, r}, Σ = {0, 1}, start p. All six cells of δ hold states from Q, and F = ∅.\nIs this a valid DFA?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Yes. F = ∅ is allowed; the machine simply recognizes the empty language ∅.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.1(d)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-015","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"yn","q":"Q = {p, q, r}, Σ = {0, 1}, start p, F = {r}. All six cells of δ are filled correctly with states of Q, but no arrow goes into q.\nIs this a valid DFA?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Yes. An unreachable state is allowed; it never affects which strings are accepted.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.1(f)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-016","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"yn","q":"Q = {p, q, r}, Σ = {0, 1}, start p, F = {r}. The diagram has an arrow from p to r labelled 01 (and is otherwise complete).\nIs this a valid DFA?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"No. DFA arrows are labelled with single symbols of Σ; a label that is a whole string like 01 is not allowed (δ takes one symbol).","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.1(e)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-017","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"yn","q":"Q = {p, q, r}, Σ = {0, 1}, F = {r}, δ complete — but both p and q are marked as start states.\nIs this a valid DFA?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"No. The 5-tuple has exactly one start state q₀ ∈ Q.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.1(g)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-018","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"mcq","q":"Q = {p, q, r}, Σ = {0, 1}. Which single fact on its own does NOT stop δ from being a valid DFA transition function?","options":["δ(r, 0) = {p, q}","δ(p, 1) = s","δ(q, 0) = q","δ(q, 1) is not given anywhere"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"A self-loop is fine. Two next states is nondeterminism, s ∉ Q is not a state of the machine, and a missing value makes δ partial — all three break the definition.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.1(a)–(c)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-019","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"tf","q":"A DFA M accepts the empty string ε exactly when its start state q₀ is an accept state.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"δ∗(q₀, ε) = q₀, so ε ∈ L(M) ⇔ q₀ ∈ F. That is why Sipser's M3 (start state accepting) accepts ε.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §4.4 Fact 3 · M&S Def. 2.2.2","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-020","chapter":"dfa","topic":"DFA 5-tuple & δ","type":"tf","q":"The extended transition function δ∗ is a sixth component that must be added to the 5-tuple of a DFA.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"δ∗ is built from δ alone by recursion; the machine is still the same 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q₀, F).","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §4.5 'Watch out'","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-021","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"The extended transition function is defined by δ∗(r, ε) = r and, for v ∈ Σ∗ and a ∈ Σ,\nδ∗(r, va) = ?","options":["δ(δ∗(r, v), a)","δ∗(δ(r, a), v)","δ(δ∗(r, a), v)","δ∗(r, v) ∪ δ(r, a)"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"First read v (reaching δ∗(r, v)), then take one more step on the last symbol a. Swapping the order applies a first, which is the wrong end of va.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §4.2 · M&S §2.2","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-022","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"M4: Q = {0, 1, 2, 3}, Σ = {a, b}, start 0, F = {0},\nδ(i, a) = (i + 1) mod 4,  δ(i, b) = (i + 2) mod 4.\nWhat is δ∗(0, ab)?","options":["1","2","0","3"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"δ∗(0, ab) = δ(δ(0, a), b) = δ(1, b) = 3. Reading b first (0 → 2 → 3) gives the same here only by luck; 2 is δ∗(0, b) alone.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 4.2(a)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-023","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"M4: Q = {0, 1, 2, 3}, Σ = {a, b}, start 0, F = {0},\nδ(i, a) = (i + 1) mod 4,  δ(i, b) = (i + 2) mod 4.\nWhat is δ∗(1, bba)?","options":["0","2","1","3"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"1 –b→ 3 –b→ 1 –a→ 2. Starting from state 0 instead of 1 is the common slip (it gives 1).","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 4.2(b)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-024","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"M4: Q = {0, 1, 2, 3}, Σ = {a, b}, start 0, F = {0},\nδ(i, a) = (i + 1) mod 4,  δ(i, b) = (i + 2) mod 4.\nWhich string does M4 accept?","options":["ab","bbb","aa","aab"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"M4 accepts w iff #a(w) + 2·#b(w) ≡ 0 (mod 4). aab: 2 + 2 = 4 ✓. ab gives 3, bbb gives 6 ≡ 2, aa gives 2.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 4.3 (length ≤ 3: ε, bb, aab, aba, baa)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-025","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"D1 over Σ = {a, b}, start s0, F = {s2}:\n         a    b\n→ s0     s1   s0\n  s1     s1   s2\n∗ s2     s1   s0\nWhat is the run of D1 on abba, and the verdict?","options":["s0, s1, s2, s0, s1 — accepted, because it passed through s2","s0, s1, s2, s0, s1 — rejected","s0, s1, s2, s1, s2 — accepted","s0, s1, s1, s2, s0 — rejected"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Only the LAST state counts: the run passes through s2 after ab, but ends in s1 ∉ F. s0,s1,s2,s1,s2 is the run of abab.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §2.3","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-026","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"D1 over Σ = {a, b}, start s0, F = {s2}:\n         a    b\n→ s0     s1   s0\n  s1     s1   s2\n∗ s2     s1   s0\nWhich string does D1 accept?","options":["aaba","abba","bab","ε"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"bab: s0, s0, s1, s2 ✓. D1 accepts exactly the strings ending with ab, so aaba and abba (end in a) and ε (s0 ∉ F) are rejected.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 2.2","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-027","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"M (prep sheet): start q0, accept q1.\nq0 –a→ q1,  q0 –b→ q2,  q1 –a→ q0,  q1 –b→ q1 (self-loop);\nq2 has no outgoing arrows.\nWhich of the following strings is NOT accepted by M?","options":["ababaabaa","abbbaa","abbbaabb","abbaabbaa"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"ababaabaa: q0 –a→ q1 –b→ q1 –a→ q0 –b→ q2, and q2 has no way out. The other three never read b while in q0 and end in q1.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q2 · Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) Q5 (A marked correct)","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q2 · Which string is NOT accepted?","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-028","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"D3 reads a binary number, most significant bit first. States r0, r1, r2 hold the remainder mod 3;\nδ(rᵢ, b) = r_j with j = (2i + b) mod 3; start and accept r0.\nWhat is δ(r2, 1)?","options":["r0","r1","undefined","r2"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"j = (2·2 + 1) mod 3 = 5 mod 3 = 2. Appending bit b to a number n gives 2n + b, which is why the rule doubles i.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §3.2","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-029","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Tracing & δ∗","type":"mcq","q":"Same D3 (binary multiples of 3, δ(rᵢ, b) = r_((2i+b) mod 3), start = accept = r0).\nWhich input does D3 accept?","options":["1110","1011","1100","1000"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"1100 = 12 ≡ 0 (run r0, r1, r0, r0, r0). 1110 = 14, 1011 = 11 and 1000 = 8 leave remainders 2, 2 and 2.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §3.2–4.3 (D3)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-030","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language of a DFA","type":"mcq","q":"M (prep sheet): start q0, accept q1.\nq0 –a→ q1,  q0 –b→ q2,  q1 –a→ q0,  q1 –b→ q1 (self-loop);\nq2 has no outgoing arrows.\nWhich expression describes L(M)?","options":["(ab∗a)∗ab∗","all strings with an odd number of a's","a(a ∪ b)∗","(ab∗a)∗"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Each visit to q0 must leave by a; loops ab∗a return to q0, and a final ab∗ ends in q1. 'Odd number of a's' is too big: ba has one a but dies in q2. (ab∗a)∗ ends in q0, which rejects.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q3","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q3 · The language L(M)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-031","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language of a DFA","type":"mcq","q":"M3 over {a, b}, start q0, F = {q3}:\n         a    b\n→ q0     q1   q2\n  q1     q1   q3\n  q2     q3   q2\n∗ q3     q3   q3\nWhat is L(M3)?","options":["Strings that start with a and end with b, or start with b and end with a","Strings containing at least one a and at least one b","Strings containing ab as a substring","Strings of length at least 2 over {a, b}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"q1 = only a's so far, q2 = only b's so far, q3 = both seen (absorbing). ba is accepted (q0, q2, q3), which rules out 'starts with a' and 'contains ab'; aa has length 2 but is rejected.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 3.2","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-032","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language of a DFA","type":"mcq","q":"M1 over {0, 1}, start q1, F = {q2}:\nδ(q1,0)=q1, δ(q1,1)=q2, δ(q2,0)=q3, δ(q2,1)=q2, δ(q3,0)=q2, δ(q3,1)=q2.\nWhat is L(M1)?","options":["Strings with at least one 1 and an even number of 0s in total","Strings that end in 1","Strings with at least one 1 and an even number of 0s after the last 1","Strings with an even number of 0s"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"q1 = no 1 yet; q2/q3 = even/odd count of 0s since the last 1 (a 1 resets to q2). 0100 is accepted although it has three 0s in total, so 'even number of 0s overall' is wrong.","source":"Sipser M1 (Fig. 1.4) · M&S §2.2 opening example","study":"book-sipser/S1-regular-languages.md — The example machines M1–M5","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-033","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language of a DFA","type":"mcq","q":"Two states over {0, 1}: on 1 go to q2, on 0 go to q1 (from either state). Start q1, F = {q1}.\nWhat language is recognized?","options":["ε and the strings that end in 0","The strings that end in 0","The strings that end in 1","The strings with an even number of 0s"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The machine is in q1 iff the last symbol was 0 — or nothing has been read. Because the start state is accepting, ε is accepted too, which the tempting 'ends in 0' leaves out.","source":"Sipser M3 (Example 1.9)","study":"book-sipser/S1-regular-languages.md — The example machines M1–M5","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-034","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language of a DFA","type":"mcq","q":"DFSM over {a, b, c}: state 1 (start, accepting) loops on b, c and goes to 2 on a; state 2 loops on a and goes back to 1 on b, c.\nWhich language does it accept?","options":["Strings that end in b or c","Strings that contain no a","Strings with an even number of a's","Strings that do not end in a"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"State 2 means 'the last symbol was a'. ε is accepted (start state is accepting) and ε does not end in a — but it does not end in b or c either, so that distractor is wrong; ab contains an a yet is accepted.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) (DFSM 'not ending in a')","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-035","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language of a DFA","type":"mcq","q":"How many states does a complete DFA need for {w ∈ {a,b}∗ : w contains at most one b}?","options":["2","3","4","1"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"q0 (no b yet, accept), q1 (one b, accept) and a dead state d for a second b, looping on a, b. Without d the machine would be partial, i.e. not a DFA by the lecture's rule.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) (DFSM 'at most one b')","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-036","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language of a DFA","type":"mcq","q":"A DFA for {w ∈ {a,b}∗ : #b(w) ≥ 2} counts b's 'but stops counting at 2'. Which accept set is right for states c0 (no b), c1 (one b), c2 (two or more)?","options":["F = {c2}","F = {c1, c2}","F = {c0, c2}","F = {c1}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Only c2 means at least two b's; c2 loops on both symbols. Accepting c1 would accept a single b.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 2.1(a)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-037","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Completeness & dead state","type":"mcq","q":"Prep Q7: a DFA over Σ = {a, b} for the strings that start with ab. How many states does the complete DFA have?","options":["3","2","4","5"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"q0 –a→ q1 –b→ q2 (accept, loops on a, b), plus a dead state qd for a wrong first or second symbol. The 3-state drawing without qd leaves arrows out, which makes it an NFA under the lecture's rule.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q7","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q7 · DFA for strings starting with \"ab\" (Σ = {a, b})","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-038","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Completeness & dead state","type":"mcq","q":"D2 recognizes the strings that start with ab: p0 –a→ p1 –b→ p2, and p2 loops on a, b (F = {p2}). The trap state is left out of the drawing.\nA student swaps accept and non-accept states to get the complement. Which string is then handled WRONGLY?","options":["ab","aba","abb","b"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"b does not start with ab, so it is in the complement, but the swapped drawing has no b-arrow from p0 and still rejects it. Complement by swapping F works only on a complete DFA: add the trap state first.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §6.8 'Watch out'","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-039","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Completeness & dead state","type":"mcq","q":"A drawing of M8 is meant to recognize {ε, a}: u0 (start, accepting) –a→ u1 (accepting), and no other arrows.\nA student swaps the accept and non-accept states. What does the swapped drawing accept?","options":["{a, b}∗ − {ε, a}","Nothing: ∅","{ε, a}","{b}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"After the swap there are no accept states at all, so it accepts nothing — but b, aa, ab are in the real complement. Fix: add a trap t for the missing arrows, then swap; F′ = {t}.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 6.4(b)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-040","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Completeness & dead state","type":"tf","q":"An empty cell in a DFA's transition table means δ is undefined there, so the machine is not yet a DFA.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Right: it does not mean 'reject'. δ : Q × Σ → Q must be total, so add a trap state that loops on every symbol and send the missing arrow there (Tutorial 3 §3.3).","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §3.3 'Watch out'","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-041","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Completeness & dead state","type":"yn","q":"M7 (complete) recognizes the strings over {a, b} that contain bb: z0 –a→ z0, z0 –b→ z1, z1 –a→ z0, z1 –b→ z2, z2 loops on a, b; F = {z2}.\nSwap F to get 'does not contain bb' (F′ = {z0, z1}). Is ε in the new language?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Yes. ε contains no bb, and the machine agrees: δ∗(z0, ε) = z0, now accepting. Swapping is safe here because M7 is complete.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 6.4(a)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-042","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Regular operations","type":"mcq","q":"Let A = {a, aa}. How many strings are in A² = AA?","options":["4","2","6","3"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"A² = {aa, aaa, aaaa}: a·aa and aa·a are the same string aaa, so 2·2 = 4 overcounts. 2 would be {xx : x ∈ A}, which is not A².","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §5.2","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-043","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Regular operations","type":"mcq","q":"C = {b, ab} and D = {a, ba}. What is CD?","options":["{ba, bba, aba, abba}","{ab, aab, bab, baab}","{bb, bab, abb, abab}","{a, b, ab, ba}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"CD = {xy : x ∈ C, y ∈ D}: b·a, b·ba, ab·a, ab·ba. The first distractor is DC (order matters), the second is C², the third is C ∪ D.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 5.1","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-044","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Regular operations","type":"mcq","q":"What is ∅∗?","options":["∅","{ε}","Σ∗","undefined"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Star allows k = 0 pieces, and joining zero strings gives ε. So ∅∗ = {ε}, a language with one string — not the empty language ∅.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §5.3","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-045","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Regular operations","type":"tf","q":"If ε ∈ B, then A ⊆ AB for every language A.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"For any x ∈ A, x = xε with ε ∈ B, so x ∈ AB.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 5.2(b)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-046","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Regular operations","type":"tf","q":"A∗ is infinite for every language A.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"∅∗ = {ε} and {ε}∗ = {ε} are finite. A∗ is infinite only if A contains a non-empty string.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 5.2(c)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-047","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Regular operations","type":"tf","q":"If A and B are finite languages, then |AB| = |A| · |B|.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"Different pairs can glue to the same string: A = {a, aa} gives |AA| = 3, not 4.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 5.2(d)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-048","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"mcq","q":"In the product construction for DFAs M1 and M2 (states Q1 × Q2, δ((r1,r2),a) = (δ1(r1,a), δ2(r2,a))), which accept set gives the INTERSECTION L(M1) ∩ L(M2)?","options":["(F1 × Q2) ∪ (Q1 × F2)","F1 × (Q2 − F2)","F1 × F2","F1 ∪ F2"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Both machines must accept, so both coordinates must be accepting. (F1×Q2) ∪ (Q1×F2) is union, F1×(Q2−F2) is difference; F1 ∪ F2 is not even a set of pairs.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §6.7 · Sipser Thm 1.25 footnote","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-049","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"mcq","q":"Same product machine. Which accept set gives the difference A − B, where A = L(M1), B = L(M2)?","options":["(Q1 − F1) × F2","F1 × (Q2 − F2)","F1 × F2","(F1 × Q2) ∪ (Q1 × F2)"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"A − B: M1 accepts and M2 does not. (Q1 − F1) × F2 is B − A — the reversed trap.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §6.7","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-050","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"mcq","q":"M5 (starts with b): s –a→ t, s –b→ y, t and y loop on a, b; F5 = {y}.\nM6 (even length): e ⇄ o on every symbol; start e, F6 = {e}.\nIn their 6-state product, which pair can never be reached from the start (s, e)?","options":["(s, o)","(t, e)","(y, o)","(s, e)"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"M5 is in s only before anything is read, and then M6 is in e. After one symbol M5 has left s for good, so (s, o) never occurs.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 6.2(b)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-051","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"mcq","q":"Product machine for A = {w : #a(w) even} (states E/O) and B = {w : w ends with b} (states N/Y); its states are (E,N), (E,Y), (O,N), (O,Y).\nWhich F makes it recognize the symmetric difference A ⊕ B (in exactly one of A, B)?","options":["{(E, Y), (O, N)}","{(O, Y)}","{(E, N), (E, Y), (O, Y)}","{(E, N), (O, Y)}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Exactly one coordinate accepts: (E,N) is in A only, (O,Y) is in B only. {(O,Y)} alone is B − A; three pairs is the union.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 6.3(b)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-052","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"mcq","q":"M1 has 3 states and M2 has 4 states over the same alphabet. How many states does their product machine have (before removing unreachable pairs)?","options":["7","64","12","81"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Q1 × Q2 has |Q1|·|Q2| = 3·4 = 12 pairs. 7 = 3 + 4 is the NFA-union count (plus a new start would be 8).","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) §6.4","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"dfa-053","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"tf","q":"If A ∪ B is regular, then A and B are both regular.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"Closure works in one direction only: for any A (even a non-regular one), A ∪ Σ∗ = Σ∗ is regular.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 6.7(b)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-054","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"tf","q":"If A is regular and w is any single string, then A ∪ {w} is regular.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"{w} is regular (a chain of |w| + 1 states spelling w, plus a trap state), and regular languages are closed under union.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 6.7(e)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-055","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"yn","q":"Are the finite languages over {a, b} closed under complement?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"No. ∅ is finite but its complement Σ∗ is infinite. One counterexample is enough to show non-closure.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 6.1(g)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-056","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Closure & product construction","type":"yn","q":"Are the languages over {a, b} in which every string has even length closed under star?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Yes. A string of A∗ is a concatenation of even-length strings, so its length is a sum of even numbers; k = 0 gives ε, length 0.","source":"Tutorial 3 (tutorials/Tutorial1/CS313_Tutorial_DFA.docx) Ex. 6.1(f)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 3 · DFAs, δ, δ∗, regular operations, closure (Lecture 3)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-057","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language algebra","type":"tf","q":"For all languages L, L₁, L₂, L₃:\n(L⁺)⁺ = L⁺","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Gluing one or more blocks, each of one or more strings of L, is just gluing one or more strings of L.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) true/false block (verdicts in the tutorial; reasons ours)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-058","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language algebra","type":"tf","q":"For all languages L, L₁, L₂, L₃:\nL∗ = L⁺ ∪ ∅","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"L⁺ ∪ ∅ = L⁺. With L = {a}: ε ∈ L∗ but ε ∉ L⁺. (The true identity is L∗ = L⁺ ∪ {ε}.)","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) true/false block (verdicts in the tutorial; reasons ours)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-059","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language algebra","type":"tf","q":"For all languages L, L₁, L₂, L₃:\n(L₁L₂L₃)∗ = L₁∗L₂∗L₃∗","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"Take L₁ = {a}, L₂ = {b}, L₃ = {c}: a ∈ L₁∗L₂∗L₃∗ but a ∉ (abc)∗.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) true/false block (verdicts in the tutorial; reasons ours)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-060","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language algebra","type":"tf","q":"For all languages L, L₁, L₂, L₃:\nL∗L = L⁺","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"⋃ over k ≥ 0 of LᵏL = ⋃ over k ≥ 1 of Lᵏ = L⁺.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) true/false block (verdicts in the tutorial; reasons ours)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-061","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language algebra","type":"tf","q":"For all languages L, L₁, L₂, L₃:\n∅L∗ = ∅","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Concatenation needs a string from each side; ∅ has none. (Note ∅L∗ = ∅ even though L∗ contains ε.)","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) true/false block (verdicts in the tutorial; reasons ours)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-062","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language algebra","type":"tf","q":"For all languages L, L₁, L₂, L₃:\nL∅ = L∗","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"L∅ = ∅, but ε ∈ L∗ always, so the two sides differ for every L.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) true/false block (verdicts in the tutorial; reasons ours)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-063","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language algebra","type":"tf","q":"For all languages L, L₁, L₂, L₃:\n((L ∪ {ε})∗(L ∪ {ε})∗)∗ = L∗","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"(L ∪ {ε})∗ = L∗, L∗L∗ = L∗ and (L∗)∗ = L∗.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) true/false block (verdicts in the tutorial; reasons ours)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"dfa-064","chapter":"dfa","topic":"Language algebra","type":"tf","q":"For all languages L, L₁, L₂, L₃:\n{ε} ∪ L⁺ = L⁺","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"Fails for any L that does not contain ε, e.g. L = {a}: ε is on the left only.","source":"Tutorial 2 (tutorials/Tutorial1/ToC_Tutorial_2.pdf) true/false block (verdicts in the tutorial; reasons ours)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 2 · answers, with the proofs it leaves out","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-001","chapter":"nfa","topic":"DFA or NFA?","type":"mcq","q":"Automaton M (prep Q1–5), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, accept q1:\nq0 –a→ q1,  q0 –b→ q2,  q1 –a→ q0,  q1 –b→ q1;\nq2 has no outgoing arrows.\nUsing Lecture 4's definition, what is M?","options":["A DFA: no state has two arrows on the same symbol and there are no ε-arrows","A DFA, because q2 behaves like a dead state anyway","Neither: an automaton with a stuck state is not a finite automaton","An NFA: q2 has no a- or b-arrow, so δ is not total"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 3: a DFA state has EXACTLY one exiting arrow per symbol, and hanging is NFA behaviour. Tutorial 2 calls this machine a 'DFSM' (hidden-dead-state convention), so on the quiz say: deterministic in spirit, but δ is partial, hence strictly an NFA; adding a dead state makes it a DFA.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q1 · Lecture 4 slide 3","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q1 · DFA or NFA?","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-002","chapter":"nfa","topic":"DFA or NFA?","type":"mcq","q":"Automaton A (prep Q9), Σ = {0, 1}, start q0, F = {q2}:\n         0          1\n→ q0     {q0, q1}   {q2}\n  q1     {q0}       {q1}\n∗ q2     ∅          {q0, q1}\nA is an NFA. Which of these is NOT a valid reason?","options":["δ(q0, 0) = {q0, q1} has two next states","q2 is an accept state that has outgoing arrows","δ(q2, 0) = ∅ has no next state","The table's entries are sets, so δ : Q × Σ → 𝒫(Q)"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Accept states may have outgoing arrows in a DFA too. Two next states, a missing next state and set-valued entries are each a correct reason (the prep answer lists all three).","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q9","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q9–10 · Automaton A (from the table)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-003","chapter":"nfa","topic":"DFA or NFA?","type":"mcq","q":"Lecture 4, slide 16: q0 loops on 0, 1; q0 –1→ q1; q1 –0,1→ q2; q2 is accepting with no arrows out.\n'Is this a DFA or NFA?' — which reason does the slide give?","options":["NFA: from q0 on a 1 it can stay in q0 or go to q1","DFA: every arrow is labelled with a symbol of Σ","NFA: it has an ε-arrow from q1 to q2","DFA: it has exactly one start state and one accept state"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Two possible next states on (q0, 1) is nondeterminism. There is no ε-arrow here. A second reason the slide omits: q2 has no outgoing arrows.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 16","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Slide 16 · DFA or NFA?","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-004","chapter":"nfa","topic":"DFA or NFA?","type":"yn","q":"Σ = {a, b}, start p, F = {q}:\np –a→ q,  p –b→ p,  q –a→ q,  q –b→ p.\nIs this a DFA?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Yes. Every state has exactly one arrow for each symbol and there are no ε-arrows, so δ : Q × Σ → Q is a total function.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 3","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Determinism vs nondeterminism (slides 2–3, 9)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-005","chapter":"nfa","topic":"DFA or NFA?","type":"yn","q":"Σ = {a, b}. Every state has exactly one a-arrow and exactly one b-arrow, and there is also one arrow labelled ε from p to q.\nIs this a DFA?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"No. Slide 3, difference 2: DFA arrows are labelled with symbols of Σ only; an ε-arrow (a free move) makes it an NFA even when every symbol arrow is present.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 3","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Determinism vs nondeterminism (slides 2–3, 9)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-006","chapter":"nfa","topic":"DFA or NFA?","type":"mcq","q":"Automaton E (Tutorial 4, Q2.2), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 –a→ q0,  q0 –a→ q1,  q1 –b→ q2,  q2 –ε→ q0.\nHow many independent reasons make E an NFA rather than a DFA?","options":["One: the ε-arrow from q2 to q0 (the rest is deterministic)","Two: q0 has two a-arrows, and q2 has an ε-arrow to q0","Three: two a-arrows from q0, an ε-arrow, and missing arrows","None: E is a DFA, because every arrow has one label"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"q0 has two a-arrows, q2 –ε→ q0 is an ε-move, and arrows are missing (q0 has no b-arrow). The tutorial: 'any one of these reasons is enough'.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q2.2(1)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-007","chapter":"nfa","topic":"DFA or NFA?","type":"mcq","q":"What is the type of the transition function of an NFA (with ε-moves), as defined in Lecture 4?","options":["δ : Q × Σ → Q","δ : Q × Σ_ε → 𝒫(Q)","δ : Q × Σ_ε → Q","δ : 𝒫(Q) × Σ → 𝒫(Q)"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Two changes from the DFA: the input may be ε (Σ_ε = Σ ∪ {ε}) and the output is a SET of states, possibly ∅. 𝒫(Q) × Σ → 𝒫(Q) is the shape of the subset-construction DFA's δ′.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 10","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Formal definition (slides 10–11)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-008","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA 5-tuple","type":"mcq","q":"In the NFA definition, what is Σ_ε?","options":["Σ ∪ {ε}","Σ∗","Σ − {ε}","{ε}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Σ_ε adds the empty string to the alphabet so that δ can be asked about ε-moves. Σ∗ is all strings, which is far too big.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 10","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Formal definition (slides 10–11)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-009","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA 5-tuple","type":"tf","q":"In an NFA, δ(r, a) = ∅ is allowed for a state r and a symbol a.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"δ maps into 𝒫(Q), and ∅ ∈ 𝒫(Q). It means that copy of the machine has no arrow to follow and hangs.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 10 · Lecture 4 slide 3","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Formal definition (slides 10–11)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-010","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA 5-tuple","type":"mcq","q":"Automaton A (prep Q9), Σ = {0, 1}, start q0, F = {q2}:\n         0          1\n→ q0     {q0, q1}   {q2}\n  q1     {q0}       {q1}\n∗ q2     ∅          {q0, q1}\nPrep Q10: in the formal definition, what is δ(q2, 1)?","options":["{q0, q2}","{q1}","∅","{q0, q1}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Read row q2, column 1. The value is a set because A is an NFA (δ : Q × Σ → 𝒫(Q)). ∅ is δ(q2, 0).","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q10","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q9–10 · Automaton A (from the table)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-011","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA 5-tuple","type":"mcq","q":"ε-NFA (prep Q11), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on a;  q0 –a→ q1;  q0 –ε→ q1;  q1 –b→ q2;  q2 loops on b.\nIn the transition table with an ε column, what is the ε entry of row q0?","options":["∅","{q0, q1}","{q1}","{q0}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"The single ε-arrow goes q0 → q1. {q0, q1} is the a entry of q0 (and the closure C_ε(q0)), which is a different thing.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q11(b)","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-012","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA 5-tuple","type":"mcq","q":"An NFA has Q = {q0, q1, q2, q3}. How many elements does 𝒫(Q), the codomain of δ, have?","options":["16","4","8","15"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"|𝒫(Q)| = 2^|Q| = 2⁴ = 16, counting ∅ and Q itself. 15 forgets ∅.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 11 · Lecture 4 slide 19","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Formal definition (slides 10–11)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-013","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA 5-tuple","type":"tf","q":"An NFA must have exactly one start state, just like a DFA.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"The 5-tuple (Q, Σ, δ, q, F) has a single start state q ∈ Q. Several 'start' possibilities are simulated with ε-arrows from that one state.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 10","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Formal definition (slides 10–11)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-014","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N (Lecture 4, slide 4), Σ = {0, 1}, start q1, F = {q4}:\n         0      1          ε\n→ q1     {q1}   {q1, q2}   ∅\n  q2     {q3}   ∅          {q3}\n  q3     ∅      {q4}       ∅\n∗ q4     {q4}   {q4}       ∅\nHow many different computations (branches) does N have on input 010, and what is the verdict?","options":["2 computations; one ends in q4, so 010 is accepted","1 computation; it ends in q1, so 010 is rejected","3 computations; one of them hangs, so 010 is rejected because of the hang","3 computations; none ends in q4, so 010 is rejected"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"q1q1q1q1 (ends q1), q1q1q2q3 (ends q3), and q1q1q2 –ε→ q3 then hangs on 0. The NFA rejects because NO branch ends in an accept state — a hanging branch alone never decides anything.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 7","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The running example N (slides 4–7, 12–13, 24)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-015","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N (Lecture 4, slide 4), Σ = {0, 1}, start q1, F = {q4}:\n         0      1          ε\n→ q1     {q1}   {q1, q2}   ∅\n  q2     {q3}   ∅          {q3}\n  q3     ∅      {q4}       ∅\n∗ q4     {q4}   {q4}       ∅\nWhich string does N reject?","options":["0110","1010","1001","0011"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"L(N) = strings containing 11 or 101. 1001 contains neither (the 1s are separated by two 0s). 0110 and 0011 contain 11, 1010 contains 101.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 4 (language verified by code to length 12)","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The running example N (slides 4–7, 12–13, 24)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-016","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N (Lecture 4, slide 4), Σ = {0, 1}, start q1, F = {q4}:\n         0      1          ε\n→ q1     {q1}   {q1, q2}   ∅\n  q2     {q3}   ∅          {q3}\n  q3     ∅      {q4}       ∅\n∗ q4     {q4}   {q4}       ∅\nWhat language does N recognize?","options":["Strings that contain 101","Strings that contain 11 or 101","Strings that contain 1011","Strings that end in 1"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"q1 guesses where the pattern starts; q2 → q3 is either the 0 of 101 or a free ε-skip, which gives 11. 'Contains 101' misses 11, which N accepts via the ε-move.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 4 · Lecture 4 slide 6","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The running example N (slides 4–7, 12–13, 24)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-017","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 13 shows N accepting w = 01100 by writing w = y₁y₂⋯yₘ with each yᵢ ∈ Σ_ε and states r₀, …, rₘ. What is m?","options":["5","6","4","7"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"w is written 0·1·ε·1·0·0: the free move q2 –ε→ q3 is a step too, so m = 6 although |w| = 5.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 13 · Lecture 4 slide 15","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — When does an NFA accept? (slides 13–15)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-018","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"tf","q":"An NFA rejects w as soon as some computation on w hangs.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"It rejects only if NO computation ends in an accept state after reading all of w. Branches that hang simply die; another branch may still accept.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 5 · Lecture 4 slide 8","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — When does an NFA accept? (slides 13–15)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-019","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"Automaton E (Tutorial 4, Q2.2), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 –a→ q0,  q0 –a→ q1,  q1 –b→ q2,  q2 –ε→ q0.\nWhich states can E be in after reading ab?","options":["{q0, q2}","{q2}","{q0, q1}","{q1, q2}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"After a: {q0, q1}. On b only q1 moves, to q2, and the ε-arrow q2 → q0 adds q0. Forgetting the closure after the move gives just {q2}.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q2.2(6)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-020","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"yn","q":"Automaton E (Tutorial 4, Q2.2), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 –a→ q0,  q0 –a→ q1,  q1 –b→ q2,  q2 –ε→ q0.\nDoes E accept ab?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Yes: the set after ab is {q0, q2}, which contains the accept state q2.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q2.2(7)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-021","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"yn","q":"Automaton E (Tutorial 4, Q2.2), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 –a→ q0,  q0 –a→ q1,  q1 –b→ q2,  q2 –ε→ q0.\nDoes E accept aba?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"No: from {q0, q2}, reading a gives {q0, q1}, which contains no accept state. (E also rejects ε: C_ε(q0) = {q0}.)","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q2.2(7)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-022","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"ε-NFA (prep Q11), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on a;  q0 –a→ q1;  q0 –ε→ q1;  q1 –b→ q2;  q2 loops on b.\nWhich string is rejected by this ε-NFA?","options":["b","aab","abb","aba"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"L = a∗b⁺. After ab every live branch is in q2, which has no a-arrow, so aba dies. b is accepted via q0 –ε→ q1 –b→ q2.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q11(i)–(j)","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-023","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"Prep Q8: an NFA for L = {w ∈ {a,b,c}∗ : w = x abcabb y for some x, y ∈ {a,b,c}∗}, built as\n'self-loop at the start, a chain spelling the pattern, self-loop at the accept state'.\nHow many states does it have?","options":["6","8","7","12"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"q0 (loops a, b, c) and then one new state per letter of abcabb: 1 + 6 = 7, with q6 accepting and looping. No extra arrows are needed for wrong letters — missing arrows are fine in an NFA.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q8","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q8 · NFA for L = {w ∈ {a,b,c}∗ : ∃x, y ∈ {a,b,c}∗ (w = x abcabb y)}","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-024","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"Same prep-Q8 NFA (q0 loops on a, b, c; chain q0 –a→ q1 –b→ q2 –c→ q3 –a→ q4 –b→ q5 –b→ q6; q6 loops). What is δ(q0, a)?","options":["{q1}","{q0}","{q0, q1}","{q0, q6}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"On a, q0 may keep reading x (stay) or guess that abcabb starts here (go to q1). That guess is exactly where the nondeterminism is.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q8","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q8 · NFA for L = {w ∈ {a,b,c}∗ : ∃x, y ∈ {a,b,c}∗ (w = x abcabb y)}","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-025","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"NFA: p loops on a; p –a→ q; q –b→ r; r –ε→ p; start p, F = {r}.\nWhich string is accepted?","options":["abab","abb","ba","aaba"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"L = (a⁺b)⁺: one or more blocks of some a's followed by one b. abb has a block with no a, ba starts with b, and aaba ends in a.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 28 (construction) · our practice P4","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Practice questions (quiz style, with solutions — all verified by code)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-026","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"mcq","q":"DFA (Lecture 4, slides 41–42), Σ = {a, b}, start q1, F = {q1, q4}:\n         a    b\n→∗ q1    q1   q2\n   q2    q3   q4\n   q3    q2   q1\n ∗ q4    q3   q4\nWhat sequence of states does the machine go through on input aabb?","options":["q1, q2, q4","q1, q1, q1, q2, q4","q1, q1, q2, q4","q1, q1, q1, q2, q3"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"a, a keep it in q1; b → q2; b → q4. Four symbols give five states (the start counts). It ends in q4 ∈ F, so aabb is accepted.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 42","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Slides 41–42 · \"Some easy questions\" (Tutorial 4's \"slide 42\")","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-027","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"yn","q":"DFA (Lecture 4, slides 41–42), Σ = {a, b}, start q1, F = {q1, q4}:\n         a    b\n→∗ q1    q1   q2\n   q2    q3   q4\n   q3    q2   q1\n ∗ q4    q3   q4\nDoes this machine accept ε?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Yes. On ε the machine never leaves q1, and q1 is an accept state.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 42","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Slides 41–42 · \"Some easy questions\" (Tutorial 4's \"slide 42\")","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-028","chapter":"nfa","topic":"NFA computation","type":"yn","q":"DFA D (Tutorial 4, Q2.1), start q1, F = {q3}:\nq1 –a→ q2, q1 –b→ q1, q2 –a→ q3, q2 –b→ q1, q3 –a→ q3, q3 –b→ q1.\nDoes D accept baab?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"No: q1 –b→ q1 –a→ q2 –a→ q3 –b→ q1, and q1 ∉ F. It passed through q3, but only the last state counts. D accepts the strings ending in aa.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q2.1(3)–(4)","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-029","chapter":"nfa","topic":"ε-closure","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N (Lecture 4, slide 4), Σ = {0, 1}, start q1, F = {q4}:\n         0      1          ε\n→ q1     {q1}   {q1, q2}   ∅\n  q2     {q3}   ∅          {q3}\n  q3     ∅      {q4}       ∅\n∗ q4     {q4}   {q4}       ∅\nWhat is C_ε(q2)?","options":["{q3}","{q2}","{q1, q2, q3}","{q2, q3}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"C_ε(r) = states reachable from r by ZERO or more ε-arrows, so it always contains r itself, plus q3 via q2 –ε→ q3. {q3} forgets r.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 24","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-030","chapter":"nfa","topic":"ε-closure","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N (Lecture 4, slide 4), Σ = {0, 1}, start q1, F = {q4}:\n         0      1          ε\n→ q1     {q1}   {q1, q2}   ∅\n  q2     {q3}   ∅          {q3}\n  q3     ∅      {q4}       ∅\n∗ q4     {q4}   {q4}       ∅\nWhat is C_ε({q1, q2})?","options":["{q1, q2}","{q2, q3}","{q1, q2, q3}","{q2, q3, q4}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"The closure of a set is the union of the closures: {q1} ∪ {q2, q3}.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 24","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-031","chapter":"nfa","topic":"ε-closure","type":"mcq","q":"ε-NFA (prep Q11), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on a;  q0 –a→ q1;  q0 –ε→ q1;  q1 –b→ q2;  q2 loops on b.\nWhat is C_ε(q0)?","options":["{q0}","{q1}","{q0, q1, q2}","{q0, q1}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"q0 itself plus q1 via the ε-arrow. q2 needs a b, so it is not in the closure.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q11(c)","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-032","chapter":"nfa","topic":"ε-closure","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N₄ (Tutorial 4, Q3.4), Σ = {a, b}, start 1, F = {3}:\n1 –a→ {1, 2};  2 –b→ 3;  3 loops on a;  3 –ε→ 2.\nWhat is C_ε(3)?","options":["{2, 3}","{3}","{2}","{1, 2, 3}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The ε-arrow LEAVES 3, so it enlarges 3's closure: {3} plus 2. It does not change C_ε(2) = {2}.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q3.4","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-033","chapter":"nfa","topic":"ε-closure","type":"mcq","q":"Same N₄ (1 –a→ {1, 2}; 2 –b→ 3; 3 loops on a; 3 –ε→ 2). What is C_ε(2)?","options":["{2, 3}","{2}","{3}","∅"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"No ε-arrow leaves 2, so its closure is just {2}. The arrow 3 –ε→ 2 points INTO 2 and only affects C_ε(3) — the classic backward-arrow trap.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q3.4","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-034","chapter":"nfa","topic":"ε-closure","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N₃ (Tutorial 4, Q3.3), Σ = {0, 1}, F = {q1, q2}:\nq0 –ε→ q1,  q0 –ε→ q2,  q1 loops on 0,  q2 loops on 1.\nWhat is the DFA's start state C_ε({q0})?","options":["{q0}","{q1, q2}","{q0, q1}","{q0, q1, q2}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"q0 plus both ε-targets. Because it contains accept states, this start state is accepting, so N₃ accepts ε.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q3.3","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-035","chapter":"nfa","topic":"ε-closure","type":"mcq","q":"NFA: p –ε→ q,  q –ε→ r,  r –a→ p. What is C_ε(p)?","options":["{p, q}","{q, r}","{p, q, r}","{p}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Zero or more ε-arrows: chains count, so p → q → r are all reachable without reading input. Stopping after one ε-step gives the wrong {p, q}.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 22 · Lecture 4 slide 23","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-036","chapter":"nfa","topic":"ε-closure","type":"tf","q":"For every state r, the ε-closure C_ε(r) contains r itself.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Zero ε-moves is allowed, so r is always reachable from r. That is why C_ε(q) is never ∅.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 22 · Lecture 4 slide 23","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-037","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"In the subset construction for an ε-NFA N = (Q, Σ, δ, q, F), what is the DFA's start state q′?","options":["{q}","C_ε({q})","Q","C_ε(F)"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Before reading anything, N can already follow ε-arrows from q, so the DFA must start in every state of C_ε({q}). {q} is only right when q has no ε-arrows.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 28","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-038","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"Subset construction with ε-arrows (Lecture 4, slide 28). For a DFA state R and symbol a, δ′(R, a) = ?","options":["⋃ over r ∈ R of C_ε(δ(r, a))","⋃ over r ∈ R of δ(C_ε(r), a)","C_ε(R) ∪ δ(R, a)","⋂ over r ∈ R of C_ε(δ(r, a))"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Move on a from every state of R, then take the ε-closure. (Equivalently C_ε(⋃ δ(r, a)).) An intersection would model 'all branches agree', which is not how an NFA works.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 28 · Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) §1.2","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-039","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"ε-NFA (prep Q11), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on a;  q0 –a→ q1;  q0 –ε→ q1;  q1 –b→ q2;  q2 loops on b.\nWhat is the start state of the equivalent DFA?","options":["{q0}","{q1}","{q0, q1}","{q0, q1, q2}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"C_ε(q0) = {q0, q1}: the NFA is already in q1 before reading input. {q0} is the trap the prep sheet warns about.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q11(d)","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-040","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"ε-NFA (prep Q11), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on a;  q0 –a→ q1;  q0 –ε→ q1;  q1 –b→ q2;  q2 loops on b.\nIn the DFA, what is δ′({q0, q1}, b)?","options":["{q1, q2}","{q2}","∅","{q0, q2}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"δ(q0, b) ∪ δ(q1, b) = ∅ ∪ {q2} = {q2}, and C_ε({q2}) = {q2}.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q11(e)","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-041","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"ε-NFA (prep Q11), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on a;  q0 –a→ q1;  q0 –ε→ q1;  q1 –b→ q2;  q2 loops on b.\nBefore pruning, the DFA has all 2³ = 8 subsets as states. How many of them are accept states?","options":["1","3","2","4"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"F′ = every subset containing q2: {q2}, {q0, q2}, {q1, q2}, {q0, q1, q2}. Marking only {q2} is the common slip.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q11(f)","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-042","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"NFA (Lecture 4, slides 29–40), Σ = {a, b}, start 1, F = {2}:\n        a      b         ε\n→ 1     {3}    ∅         {2}\n∗ 2     {1}    ∅         ∅\n  3     {2}    {2, 3}    ∅\nWhat is δ′({2}, a)?","options":["{1, 2}","{1}","{2}","{1, 2, 3}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"δ(2, a) = {1}, then the closure: the ε-arrow 1 → 2 pulls 2 back in, so C_ε({1}) = {1, 2}. Forgetting the closure after the move gives {1}.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 31–32","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The worked conversion (slides 29–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-043","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"NFA (Lecture 4, slides 29–40), Σ = {a, b}, start 1, F = {2}:\n        a      b         ε\n→ 1     {3}    ∅         {2}\n∗ 2     {1}    ∅         ∅\n  3     {2}    {2, 3}    ∅\nWhat is δ′({1, 3}, b)?","options":["∅","{2, 3}","{2}","{1, 2, 3}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"C_ε(δ(1, b)) ∪ C_ε(δ(3, b)) = C_ε(∅) ∪ C_ε({2, 3}) = {2, 3}. State 1 contributing nothing does not make the whole result ∅.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 31–32","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The worked conversion (slides 29–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-044","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"NFA (Lecture 4, slides 29–40), Σ = {a, b}, start 1, F = {2}:\n        a      b         ε\n→ 1     {3}    ∅         {2}\n∗ 2     {1}    ∅         ∅\n  3     {2}    {2, 3}    ∅\nWhat is the start state of the DFA?","options":["{1}","{2}","{1, 2}","{1, 3}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"q′ = C_ε({1}) = {1, 2} because of 1 –ε→ 2. It is also an accept state, since it contains 2 ∈ F.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 30 · Lecture 4 slide 33","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The worked conversion (slides 29–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-045","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"NFA (Tutorial 4 §1.3), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\n         a      b          ε\n→ q0     {q0}   ∅          {q1}\n  q1     ∅      {q1, q2}   ∅\n∗ q2     {q2}   ∅          ∅\nWith q′ = {q0, q1}, what is δ′({q0, q1}, b)?","options":["{q1, q2}","{q2}","∅","{q0, q1, q2}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"q0 has no b-arrow, q1 –b→ {q1, q2}; the closure adds nothing. This new set gets its own row and is accepting.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) §1.3","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-046","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N₂ (Tutorial 4, Q3.2), Σ = {0, 1}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on 0, 1;  q0 –1→ q1;  q1 –0,1→ q2.\nHow many DFA states are reachable from {q0} after the subset construction?","options":["3","8","5","4"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"{q0}, {q0, q1}, {q0, q2}, {q0, q1, q2}: the DFA must remember the last two symbols (second-from-last = 1). 8 is the count before pruning.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q3.2 · Lecture 4 slide 16","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-047","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"yn","q":"NFA N₁ (Tutorial 4, Q3.1): q0 loops on 0, 1; q0 –1→ q1; start q0, F = {q1}.\nRun the converted DFA ({q0} and {q0, q1}). Does N₁ accept 0110?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"No: {q0} –0→ {q0} –1→ {q0, q1} –1→ {q0, q1} –0→ {q0}, which is not accepting. N₁ accepts the strings ending in 1 (0101 yes).","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q3.1","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-048","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"N₃ (Tutorial 4, Q3.3): q0 –ε→ q1, q0 –ε→ q2, q1 loops on 0, q2 loops on 1; F = {q1, q2}.\nWhich input does N₃ reject?","options":["01","000","ε","11"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"DFA: {q0,q1,q2} –0→ {q1} –1→ ∅. N₃ accepts only 0s or only 1s, and ε (the start set is accepting).","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q3.3","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-049","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"N₄ (Tutorial 4, Q3.4): 1 –a→ {1, 2}; 2 –b→ 3; 3 loops on a; 3 –ε→ 2; start 1, F = {3}.\nWhich input does N₄ accept?","options":["ba","bab","ε","aab"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"{1} –a→ {1,2} –a→ {1,2} –b→ C_ε({3}) = {2,3}, accepting. N₄ accepts strings that start with a and contain a b; ba starts with b, ε never reaches 3.","source":"Tutorial 4 (tutorials/Tutorial2/CS313_Tutorial_4_NFA_to_DFA.docx) Q3.4","study":"quizzes/T-tutorials-2-4.md — Tutorial 4 · NFA → DFA (the closest thing to the new quiz question)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-050","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Subset construction","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N (Lecture 4, slide 4), Σ = {0, 1}, start q1, F = {q4}:\n         0      1          ε\n→ q1     {q1}   {q1, q2}   ∅\n  q2     {q3}   ∅          {q3}\n  q3     ∅      {q4}       ∅\n∗ q4     {q4}   {q4}       ∅\nConverting N to a DFA (q′ = {q1}), what is δ′({q1}, 1)?","options":["{q1, q2}","{q1, q2, q3}","{q2, q3}","{q1, q2, q3, q4}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"δ(q1, 1) = {q1, q2}, then close: C_ε(q2) = {q2, q3}. The closure is taken AFTER the move, which is why q3 appears.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 24 + Lecture 4 slide 28 · our practice P3","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Practice questions (quiz style, with solutions — all verified by code)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-051","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Pruning & the ∅ state","type":"mcq","q":"ε-NFA (prep Q11), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on a;  q0 –a→ q1;  q0 –ε→ q1;  q1 –b→ q2;  q2 loops on b.\nAfter pruning (Lecture 4's way: all 8 subsets, then remove what is unreachable), how many DFA states remain?","options":["2","4","3","8"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"{q0, q1} (start), {q2} (accept) and ∅ (dead, reached by a from {q2}). Lecture 4's pruned DFA keeps ∅; deleting it gives the tempting 2.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q11(h) · Lecture 4 slide 39","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-052","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Pruning & the ∅ state","type":"mcq","q":"NFA (Lecture 4, slides 29–40), Σ = {a, b}, start 1, F = {2}:\n        a      b         ε\n→ 1     {3}    ∅         {2}\n∗ 2     {1}    ∅         ∅\n  3     {2}    {2, 3}    ∅\nAfter the 8-row table is pruned, how many states does the final DFA (slide 39) have?","options":["4","3","5","8"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"{1, 2}, {1, 2, 3}, {2, 3} and ∅. ∅ is reachable (b from {1, 2}) and the lecture keeps it as the dead state with an a, b self-loop.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 39","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The worked conversion (slides 29–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-053","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Pruning & the ∅ state","type":"tf","q":"In Lecture 4's convention, the ∅ state is always deleted when the subset-construction DFA is pruned.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"Slide 39 keeps ∅ (with its a, b self-loop) because it is reachable. Pruning removes only states not reachable from the start; ∅ goes only if it is unreachable.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 39","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The worked conversion (slides 29–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-054","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Pruning & the ∅ state","type":"mcq","q":"What is the rule for pruning the DFA produced by the subset construction?","options":["Remove every state that has no incoming arrows","Remove the ∅ state and every non-accepting state","Remove every state that cannot reach an accept state","Remove every state that cannot be reached from the start state"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Reachability from q′ is the rule. 'No incoming arrows' (slide 38's first check) misses states such as {2} and {3}, which have incoming arrows but only from other unreachable states.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 38–39","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The worked conversion (slides 29–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-055","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Pruning & the ∅ state","type":"mcq","q":"NFA (Lecture 4, slides 29–40), Σ = {a, b}, start 1, F = {2}:\n        a      b         ε\n→ 1     {3}    ∅         {2}\n∗ 2     {1}    ∅         ∅\n  3     {2}    {2, 3}    ∅\nIn the full 8-row DFA table, which state HAS an incoming arrow but is still removed by pruning?","options":["{1, 2, 3}","{2, 3}","{2}","∅"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"{2}'s only incoming arrow comes from {3}, whose only incoming arrow comes from {1}, which nothing reaches. The other three are reachable from {1, 2} and stay.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 38–39","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The worked conversion (slides 29–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-056","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Pruning & the ∅ state","type":"yn","q":"Slide-16 NFA (q0 loops on 0, 1; q0 –1→ q1; q1 –0,1→ q2; F = {q2}), converted the lecture's way.\nDoes the pruned DFA contain the ∅ state?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"No. From {q0} every move keeps q0 in the set (q0 loops on both symbols), so ∅ is never reached and is pruned. Keep ∅ only when it is reachable.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 16 · our practice P2","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Practice questions (quiz style, with solutions — all verified by code)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-057","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Pruning & the ∅ state","type":"yn","q":"ε-NFA (prep Q11), Σ = {a, b}, start q0, F = {q2}:\nq0 loops on a;  q0 –a→ q1;  q0 –ε→ q1;  q1 –b→ q2;  q2 loops on b.\nIn the pruned DFA, written Lecture 4's way, should ∅ be kept as a state?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Yes. ∅ is reachable ({q2} –a→ ∅), so it stays as the dead state with a, b self-loops, exactly as slide 39 keeps ∅. Leaving it out would make the DFA partial.","source":"Quiz 1 prep sheet (quizzes/CS313_F26_Quiz1_PrepQuestions.pdf) Q11(h) · Lecture 4 slide 39","study":"quizzes/Q1-prep-solutions.md — Q11 · ε-NFA → DFA (new this year)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"nfa-058","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Pruning & the ∅ state","type":"mcq","q":"An NFA has 3 states. How many states does its subset-construction DFA have BEFORE pruning (the lecture's layout)?","options":["3","8","6","9"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Q′ = 𝒫(Q), so |Q′| = 2³ = 8 — including ∅ and the full set. 9 = 3² and 6 = 2·3 are arithmetic slips.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 19 · Lecture 4 slide 30","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-059","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Equivalence & errata","type":"tf","q":"A language is regular if and only if some NFA accepts it.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Slide 28's theorem. Every DFA is an NFA (one direction), and the subset construction turns any NFA into a DFA (the other).","source":"Lecture 4 slide 28","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-060","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Equivalence & errata","type":"tf","q":"Every DFA is also an NFA.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Set δ_N(r, a) = {δ(r, a)} and δ_N(r, ε) = ∅. Nondeterminism generalizes determinism (slide 2).","source":"Lecture 4 slide 2","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Determinism vs nondeterminism (slides 2–3, 9)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-061","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Equivalence & errata","type":"tf","q":"Slide 20 says an NFA without ε-arrows 'is not a true NFA'. That statement is correct.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"Wrong (errata). Several arrows on one symbol, or missing arrows, already make a machine nondeterministic. The no-ε case is just the easier half of the proof.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 20","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — ⚠️ Lecture 4 slide errata","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-062","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Equivalence & errata","type":"tf","q":"The DFA state named {1, 2, 3} means the DFA is in three states at the same time.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"A DFA is always in exactly one state; {1, 2, 3} is that state's NAME (slides 34–36 rename it Y). It records which NFA states are possible.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 36","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-063","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Equivalence & errata","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 26 reads: 'for the special case we proved (with ε-transitions) …'. What should it say?","options":["Nothing — 'with ε-transitions' is correct as written","'with multiple start states' — N had several starts","'without ε-transitions' — the first, simpler case assumed no ε-arrows","'with no accept states' — the case where F = ∅"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 18 states the special case as the NFA without ε-arrows; slides 21–27 then add ε via the closure.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 26 · Lecture 4 slide 18","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — ⚠️ Lecture 4 slide errata","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-064","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Equivalence & errata","type":"mcq","q":"NFA (Lecture 4, slides 29–40), Σ = {a, b}, start 1, F = {2}:\n        a      b         ε\n→ 1     {3}    ∅         {2}\n∗ 2     {1}    ∅         ∅\n  3     {2}    {2, 3}    ∅\nWhat language does this NFA (and its 4-state DFA) accept? The slides never say.","options":["Strings that do not start with b and do not contain bab","Strings that do not start with b (any continuation allowed)","Strings that do not contain bb anywhere in them","Strings that are empty or end in the symbol a"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The only way into ∅ is reading b in {1, 2}, which is the state at the start and right after …ba. abab starts with a but is rejected, so 'does not start with b' is too big; abb is accepted although it contains bb.","source":"Lecture 4 slide 39 (language verified by code to length 12)","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — The worked conversion (slides 29–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"nfa-065","chapter":"nfa","topic":"Equivalence & errata","type":"tf","q":"In the worst case, the DFA equivalent to an n-state NFA needs 2ⁿ states.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"Q′ = 𝒫(Q) has 2ⁿ elements, and there are NFAs whose DFAs need nearly all of them. (In the examples here pruning leaves far fewer.)","source":"Lecture 4 slide 19 · M&S §2.5","study":"04-nondeterministic-finite-automata.md — Equivalence of DFAs and NFAs (slides 17–28)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-001","chapter":"rex","topic":"Regular operations","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 6: A = {0, 01}, B = {1, 10}. What is AB?","options":["{01, 010, 011, 0110}","{0, 01, 1, 10}","{01, 10}","{01, 010, 011, 0110, 10, 101}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"AB = {xy : x ∈ A, y ∈ B}: 0·1, 0·10, 01·1, 01·10. {0, 01, 1, 10} is A ∪ B.","source":"Lecture 5 slides 6–7","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — The class exercise","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-002","chapter":"rex","topic":"Regular operations","type":"mcq","q":"A = {0, 01}. Which string is NOT in A*?","options":["0101","001","011","ε"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Every 1 in A* comes right after the 0 of a '01' piece, so A* never contains 11. ε ∈ A* (k = 0), 0101 = 01·01, 001 = 0·01.","source":"Lecture 5 slides 7–8","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — The class exercise","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-003","chapter":"rex","topic":"Regular operations","type":"mcq","q":"A = {a, ab}, B = {b, ε}. How many strings are in AB?","options":["4","3","2","6"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"a·b and ab·ε both give ab, so AB = {a, ab, abb}: 3 strings. Count each string once.","source":"practice (TQ1-prep §1.3)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-004","chapter":"rex","topic":"Regular operations","type":"tf","q":"For every language A (even A = ∅), ε ∈ A*.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"A* = {x₁…x_k : k ≥ 0, xᵢ ∈ A}; k = 0 gives ε. That is why ∅* = {ε}.","source":"Lecture 5 slides 5, 29","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Recap and the three regular operations","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-005","chapter":"rex","topic":"Closure constructions","type":"mcq","q":"In the NFA for A₁A₂ (concatenation, slide 14), which states accept?","options":["F₁ ∪ F₂","F₂ only","F₁ only","A new state q₀ plus F₂"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Only F₂: M₁'s accept states lose their status and get ε-arrows to q₂. No new state is added for concatenation.","source":"Lecture 5 slides 14–15","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Closure","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-006","chapter":"rex","topic":"Closure constructions","type":"mcq","q":"Why does the star construction (slide 17) add a NEW start state q₀ instead of making q₁ accepting?","options":["To accept ε without letting the machine accept extra strings that pass back through q₁","Because an NFA may have only one accept state","Because q₁ must not have outgoing ε-arrows","To make the machine deterministic"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"If q₁ has incoming arrows, making it accepting can accept strings outside A*. The new q₀ has no incoming arrows, so it only adds ε.","source":"Lecture 5 slide 17 · M&S Exercise 2.7","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Closure","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-007","chapter":"rex","topic":"Closure constructions","type":"mcq","q":"Union construction (slide 11): what is δ(q₀, a) for a symbol a ≠ ε?","options":["{q₁, q₂}","∅","{q₀}","δ₁(q₁, a) ∪ δ₂(q₂, a)"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The new start state only has ε-arrows (to q₁ and q₂); on a real symbol it goes nowhere: ∅.","source":"Lecture 5 slide 11","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Closure","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-008","chapter":"rex","topic":"Closure constructions","type":"mcq","q":"Complement of a regular language: which construction is correct?","options":["Swap accept and non-accept states of a DFA for A","Swap accept and non-accept states of any NFA for A","Reverse all arrows of a DFA for A","Add a new accepting start state"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Only a DFA has exactly one computation per string. With an NFA a string can have accepting and rejecting paths, so swapping fails.","source":"Lecture 5 slide 40 · M&S Ex 2.9","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Closure under complement and intersection","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-009","chapter":"rex","topic":"Writing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 20: which regex describes {w ∈ {0,1}* : w contains exactly two 0s}?","options":["1*01*01*","(0∪1)*0(0∪1)*0(0∪1)*","0*10*1","(1*01*)*"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Exactly two 0s with any 1s around them. (0∪1)*0(0∪1)*0(0∪1)* is 'at least two 0s' (slide 21).","source":"Lecture 5 slides 20–21","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Examples","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-010","chapter":"rex","topic":"Writing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 22 asks: regex for {w ∈ {0,1}* : 1011 is a substring}?","options":["1011(0∪1)*","(0∪1)*1011(0∪1)*","(0∪1)*1011","(0∪1011)*"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"'Contains X' = (0∪1)*X(0∪1)*. The other options force 1011 to be a prefix or a suffix.","source":"Lecture 5 slides 22–23","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Examples","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-011","chapter":"rex","topic":"Writing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 24 asks: regex for {w ∈ {0,1}* : |w| is even}?","options":["(0∪1)(0∪1)*","((0∪1)(0∪1))*","(00∪11)*","(0∪1)*(0∪1)*"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Star of a 2-symbol block. (00∪11)* misses 01 and 10; the others allow odd lengths.","source":"Lecture 5 slides 24–25","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Examples","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-012","chapter":"rex","topic":"Writing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Tutorial: regex for {w ∈ {0,1}* : w does NOT have 001 as a substring}?","options":["(1∪01)*0*","(0∪1)*(1∪0)*","1*(0∪01)*","(0∪10)*1*"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Once 00 appears, a later 1 would create 001, so: blocks 1 and 01, then a tail of 0s. 1*(0∪01)* contains 001 (it matches 001 itself).","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-013","chapter":"rex","topic":"Writing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Tutorial: regex for {w ∈ {0,1}* : w does NOT have 100 as a substring}?","options":["(0∪1)*100(0∪1)*","0*(1∪10)*","(1∪10)*0*","0*(1∪01)*"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"After the first 1, every 0 must be followed by a 1 or end the string. (1∪10)*0* allows 100 (it matches 100 itself).","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 3","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-014","chapter":"rex","topic":"Writing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Regex for {w ∈ {0,1}* : w does not contain 11}?","options":["(0∪10)*(ε∪1)","(0∪1)*0(0∪1)*","(01∪10)*","0*(10)*"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Blocks 0 and 10 never create 11; allow one final 1. (01∪10)* misses 0; 0*(10)* misses 1 and 010.","source":"practice (TQ1-prep W5)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-015","chapter":"rex","topic":"Writing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Regex for {w ∈ {0,1}* : w has an even number of 0s}?","options":["(00)*","1*(01*01*)*","(1∪00)*","(0∪1)*00(0∪1)*"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Pairs of 0s with any 1s between them. (1∪00)* misses 010 (two 0s, not adjacent); (00)* has no 1s at all.","source":"practice (TQ1-prep W7)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-016","chapter":"rex","topic":"Writing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Tutorial: L = {w ∈ {a,b}* : the first a in w is immediately followed by two b's}. Which regex?","options":["abb(a∪b)*","b*(ε∪abb(a∪b)*)","b*abb(a∪b)*","(b∪abb)*"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Strings with no a at all (b*) are in L, so the ε branch is needed. b*abb(a∪b)* misses bbb.","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 3","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-017","chapter":"rex","topic":"Describing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"What does (0∪1)01* describe (slide 19)?","options":["Strings whose 2nd symbol is 0 and whose remaining symbols are all 1","Strings that contain 01","Strings that end in 1","Strings that start with 0 and end with 1"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"First symbol anything, second symbol 0, then only 1s: {00, 001, …, 10, 101, …}.","source":"Lecture 5 slide 19","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Examples","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-018","chapter":"rex","topic":"Describing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"Tutorial 3b: what does (a∪aab)(a∪b)*(ba∪bba) describe?","options":["Length ≥ 3, starts and ends with a","Starts with a and ends with ba","Contains aab and bba","Starts with aab and ends with bba"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The sheet says 'length ≥ 3, starts and ends with a', but that is WRONG: the expression always ends in ba, so aaa is not generated. Correct: a(a∪b)*ba.","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 3 (sheet's answer corrected)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-019","chapter":"rex","topic":"Describing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"FSM M (tutorial) accepts exactly b*a*. Which description is correct?","options":["Strings that do not contain the substring ba","Strings that do not contain the substring ab","Strings that start with b and end with a","Strings with more b's than a's"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"All b's come before all a's ⟺ no 'ab'. The sheet's extra '(or do not contain ba)' is wrong: M accepts ba and rejects ab.","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 2 (sheet's note corrected)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-020","chapter":"rex","topic":"Describing a regex","type":"mcq","q":"What does (a∪b)*a(a∪b)(a∪b) describe?","options":["Strings containing aab","The third symbol from the end is a","The third symbol is a","Strings of length exactly 3 starting with a"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The a is followed by exactly two arbitrary symbols at the end, so it is the third symbol from the end.","source":"practice (TQ1-prep §1.3)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-021","chapter":"rex","topic":"Identities & simplifying","type":"mcq","q":"What language does 1*∅ describe (slide 30)?","options":["{ε}","∅","1*","{1}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"L(1*∅) = {xy : x ∈ L(1*), y ∈ ∅}: no y exists, so it is empty. Concatenation with ∅ gives ∅ (identity 1).","source":"Lecture 5 slides 29–30","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Language examples","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-022","chapter":"rex","topic":"Identities & simplifying","type":"mcq","q":"What is ∅*?","options":["∅","{ε}","Σ*","Undefined"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Identity 9: ∅* = ε. The star always includes the empty concatenation (k = 0).","source":"Lecture 5 slides 29, 33","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — The 16 identities","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-023","chapter":"rex","topic":"Identities & simplifying","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 34: (0 ∪ ε)1* = 01* ∪ ε1* = 01* ∪ 1*. Which identities are used, in order?","options":["7 (right distributive), then 2 (ε is the concatenation identity)","6, then 3","5, then 4","11, then 14"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(R₁ ∪ R₂)R₃ = R₁R₃ ∪ R₂R₃ is identity 7; εR = R is identity 2.","source":"Lecture 5 slide 34","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — The 16 identities","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-024","chapter":"rex","topic":"Identities & simplifying","type":"mcq","q":"Tutorial: simplify (∅* ∪ b)b*.","options":["∅","b*","b⁺","(ε ∪ b)"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"∅* = ε (identity 9), and (ε ∪ b)b* = b* (identity 13).","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 2","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-025","chapter":"rex","topic":"Identities & simplifying","type":"mcq","q":"Tutorial: simplify (a ∪ b)*a* ∪ b.","options":["(a ∪ b)*","a*b","(a ∪ b)*a*","a* ∪ b"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(a ∪ b)*a* is already everything over {a, b}, and b is included in it.","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 2","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-026","chapter":"rex","topic":"Identities & simplifying","type":"mcq","q":"Simplify (a ∪ ε)*b ∪ b.","options":["a*b","(a ∪ b)*","a⁺b","b"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(ε ∪ a)* = a* (identity 11), then a*b ∪ b = a*b (identity 14).","source":"practice (TQ1 mock 1c)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-027","chapter":"rex","topic":"True or false","type":"tf","q":"(ab)*a = a(ba)*","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Identity 15 (shifting) with R₁ = a, R₂ = b. Both are a, aba, ababa, …","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 4 · slide 33","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-028","chapter":"rex","topic":"True or false","type":"tf","q":"(a ∪ b)*b(a ∪ b)* ∪ (a ∪ b)*a(a ∪ b)* = (a ∪ b)*","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"False: ε is in (a ∪ b)* but the left side needs at least one symbol. The left side is (a ∪ b)⁺.","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 4","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-029","chapter":"rex","topic":"True or false","type":"tf","q":"(a ∪ b)* = a* ∪ b*","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"False: ab ∈ (a ∪ b)* but ab ∉ a* ∪ b*.","source":"practice","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-030","chapter":"rex","topic":"True or false","type":"tf","q":"(a*b*)* = (a ∪ b)*","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"True: each single symbol a or b is in a*b*, so any string is a concatenation of pieces from a*b*.","source":"practice (TQ1 mock 1d)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-031","chapter":"rex","topic":"True or false","type":"tf","q":"(ab)* = a*b*","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"False: a ∈ a*b* but a ∉ (ab)*. Also abab ∈ (ab)* but not in a*b*.","source":"practice","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"rex-032","chapter":"rex","topic":"Automata ↔ regex","type":"mcq","q":"Tutorial DFA: q0 (start, accepting) loops on a, q0 –b→ q1, q1 loops on a, q1 –b→ q0. Which regex?","options":["(a ∪ ba*b)*","a*ba*b","(a ∪ b)*b(a ∪ b)*b","(ab)*"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"It accepts strings with an even number of b's: from q0 read a, or read b, a's, b back to q0.","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-033","chapter":"rex","topic":"Automata ↔ regex","type":"yn","q":"Tutorial DFA (even number of b's, start state accepting): is abbba accepted?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"No: q0 –a→ q0 –b→ q1 –b→ q0 –b→ q1 –a→ q1 ends in non-accepting q1 (three b's).","source":"Tutorial_RegEx p. 1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §1.2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-034","chapter":"rex","topic":"Automata ↔ regex","type":"mcq","q":"Slides 36–39 build an NFA for (ab ∪ a)*. In the final machine M₅, which states accept?","options":["Only the new start state","The new start state and the accept states of the ab and a branches","Only the accept states of the ab and a branches","Every state"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Star keeps F₁ and adds the new accepting start state (so ε is accepted). Accepting: n, p₄, q₂.","source":"Lecture 5 slides 38–39","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — The worked example","origin":"professor"},{"id":"rex-035","chapter":"rex","topic":"Automata ↔ regex","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 35's theorem says…","options":["Every language is regular","L is regular ⟺ some regular expression describes L","Every NFA has an equivalent regex but not conversely","Regular expressions describe exactly the context-free languages"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Both directions: regex → regular (M&S §2.8.1) and regular → regex (§2.8.2, language equations).","source":"Lecture 5 slide 35","study":"05-regular-expressions.md — Regular expressions = regular languages","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-001","chapter":"pump","topic":"Statement","type":"mcq","q":"In the pumping lemma, which condition is NOT part of it?","options":["y ≠ ε","|xy| ≤ p","|z| ≤ p","xyⁱz ∈ A for all i ≥ 0"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"The three conditions are y ≠ ε, |xy| ≤ p, and xyⁱz ∈ A for all i ≥ 0. z can be any length.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-002","chapter":"pump","topic":"Statement","type":"mcq","q":"The correct quantifier order of the pumping lemma is…","options":["∃p ∀s ∃x,y,z ∀i","∀p ∃s ∀x,y,z ∃i","∃p ∃s ∀x,y,z ∀i","∀p ∀s ∃x,y,z ∃i"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"There EXISTS a pumping length p; for EVERY long s ∈ A there EXIST x, y, z; and for ALL i ≥ 0 the pumped string is in A.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-003","chapter":"pump","topic":"Statement","type":"tf","q":"The pumping lemma allows i = 0 (pumping down).","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Condition 3 is for all i ≥ 0, so i = 0 (deleting y) is allowed, and it is essential for {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m > n}.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-004","chapter":"pump","topic":"Statement","type":"tf","q":"If a language satisfies the pumping conditions, it is regular.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"False: the converse of the lemma does not hold (M&S Ex 2.30). The lemma can only prove NON-regularity.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1 · Ex 2.30","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-005","chapter":"pump","topic":"Statement","type":"mcq","q":"Why is the pumping lemma true?","options":["A DFA with p states repeats a state within the first p symbols (pigeonhole), and y is the loop","Every regular language is finite","Regular expressions use at most p operators","Strings longer than p are rejected by every DFA"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Reading p symbols visits p + 1 states, so one repeats; the input read around that loop is y, and the loop can be taken any number of times.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1 proof","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-006","chapter":"pump","topic":"Who chooses what","type":"mcq","q":"In a non-regularity proof, which do YOU get to choose?","options":["p and the split xyz","s and i","p and i","the split xyz and s"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"p and the split are given by the lemma (handle all of them); you pick the string s (in terms of p) and the value of i.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-007","chapter":"pump","topic":"Who chooses what","type":"mcq","q":"A student writes 'let p = 3' in a pumping proof. What is wrong?","options":["Nothing, any p works","p is given by the lemma and unknown; the proof must work for every p","p must be even","p must be larger than |s|"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The lemma only says some p exists. Fixing p = 3 proves nothing about the actual pumping length.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-008","chapter":"pump","topic":"Who chooses what","type":"mcq","q":"For s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ, what does |xy| ≤ p tell you?","options":["y contains both 0s and 1s","y consists only of 0s","y consists only of 1s","y = s"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The first p symbols are all 0s and xy fits inside them, so y = 0ᵏ with 1 ≤ k ≤ p.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-009","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"Proving {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m > n} non-regular with s = 0^{p+1}1ᵖ: which i works?","options":["i = 2","i = 0","i = 1","i = p"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Pump down: xz = 0^{p+1−k}1ᵖ has at most p zeros, so m > n fails. i = 2 adds 0s and stays in the language; i = 1 is s itself.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1 · M&S example 4","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-010","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"For A = {w : #0(w) = #1(w)}, which choice of s works?","options":["s = (01)ᵖ","s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ","s = 01","s = 1ᵖ0"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"With (01)ᵖ the split may put y = 01, and every pumped string stays balanced. With 0ᵖ1ᵖ, y = 0ᵏ and xy²z has too many 0s. 01 is shorter than p; 1ᵖ0 is not in A.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1 · M&S example 2","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-011","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"For A = {ww : w ∈ {0,1}*}, which s gives a working proof?","options":["s = 0^{2p}","s = 0ᵖ1 0ᵖ1","s = (01)ᵖ","s = 1ᵖ"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"0^{2p} fails: if y has even length, every pumped string is still of the form ww. (01)ᵖ is not even in A when p is odd, and 1ᵖ likewise. 0ᵖ10ᵖ1 is in A and forces y into the first block of 0s, which breaks the match.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1 · M&S example 3","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-012","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"For A = {1^{n²} : n ≥ 0}, s = 1^{p²} and i = 2. Why is xy²z ∉ A?","options":["Its length p² + k lies strictly between p² and (p+1)²","Its length is odd","It contains a 0","Its length is 2p²"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"1 ≤ k ≤ p gives p² < p² + k ≤ p² + p < (p+1)², so the length is not a perfect square.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1 · M&S example 5","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-013","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"For A = {1ⁿ : n prime} with s = 1ⁿ (n prime, n ≥ p), y = 1ᵏ: which i gives a contradiction?","options":["i = 0","i = n + 1","i = 2","i = 1"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Length n + nk = n(1 + k), a product of two numbers ≥ 2, so not prime. i may depend on n.","source":"M&S Thm 2.9.1 · M&S example 6","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-014","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"yn","q":"Is {aⁿbᵐ : n, m ≥ 0} regular?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Yes: it is a*b*. There is no link between n and m to count.","source":"practice","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2.6","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-015","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"yn","q":"Is {aⁿbⁿ : 0 ≤ n ≤ 100} regular?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Yes: it is finite, and every finite language is regular. Pumping only bites when n is unbounded.","source":"practice","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2.6","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-016","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"yn","q":"Is {w ∈ {0,1}* : #01(w) = #10(w)} regular?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Yes: it is ε plus the strings whose first and last symbols are equal. Counting does not automatically mean non-regular.","source":"M&S §2.9 example 7","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2.6","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-017","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"yn","q":"Is {0ⁿ1 0ⁿ : n ≥ 0} regular?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"No: s = 0ᵖ10ᵖ, y = 0ᵏ in the first block, and xy²z has unequal blocks.","source":"practice (TQ1 mock)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-018","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"mcq","q":"How can you prove {0ᵐ1ⁿ : m ≠ n} is not regular most easily?","options":["Pump s = 0ᵖ1ᵖ with i = 2","Closure: its complement ∩ 0*1* = {0ⁿ1ⁿ}, which is not regular","Show it is infinite","Give a DFA for it"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"0ᵖ1ᵖ is not in the language. Closure under complement and intersection (slides 40–41) turns the problem into {0ⁿ1ⁿ}.","source":"Lecture 5 slides 40–41 · M&S Ex 2.21","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §2.5","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-019","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"mcq","q":"A = {aⁱbʲ : i ≤ j}. With s = aᵖbᵖ, which i gives the contradiction?","options":["i = 0","i = 2","i = 1","No i works; choose another s"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"xy²z = a^{p+k}bᵖ has more a's than b's. i = 0 gives a^{p−k}bᵖ, which is still in A.","source":"practice (TQ1 mock Q2)","study":"quizzes/TQ1-prep.md — §3","origin":"practice"},{"id":"pump-020","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"L = {wcw : w ∈ {a,b}*}. With s = aᵖcaᵖ, what does i = 0 give, and is it in L?","options":["a^{p−k}caᵖ — not in L, the two sides differ","aᵖcaᵖ — still in L","a^{p−k}ca^{p−k} — still in L","aᵖ — not in L, the c is deleted"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"y = aᵏ lies in the first block (|xy| ≤ p), so only the left side shrinks. The c is never touched because it sits at position p + 1.","source":"Tutorial 6 (CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx) Task 2","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — Tutorial tasks","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-021","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"L = {w : #a(w) = 2·#b(w)}. Which string and pump does the tutorial use?","options":["s = (aab)ᵖ, i = 2","s = a^{2p}bᵖ, i = 0","s = aᵖbᵖ, i = 2","s = bᵖa^{2p}, i = 0"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"a^{2p}bᵖ is in L and starts with p a's, so y = aᵏ; xz has 2p − k a's ≠ 2p. aᵖbᵖ is not even in L, and (aab)ᵖ lets the opponent pump y = aab.","source":"Tutorial 6 (CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx) Task 3(a)","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — Tutorial tasks","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-022","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"mcq","q":"Why is {aⁿbⁿ : 0 ≤ n ≤ 100} regular?","options":["It passes the pumping lemma","It is finite, and every finite language is regular","Because aⁿbⁿ is regular","It is not regular"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"101 strings → finite → regular (a DFA counts to 100). Passing the pumping test never proves regularity.","source":"Tutorial 6 (CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx) Task 4(a)","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — Regular or not","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-023","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"tf","q":"{w ∈ {a,b}* : #a(w) and #b(w) are both even} is regular.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":true,"why":"A 4-state product DFA tracks the two parities (Tutorial 3 product construction).","source":"Tutorial 6 (CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx) Task 4(b)","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — Regular or not","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-024","chapter":"pump","topic":"Regular or not?","type":"mcq","q":"{a^N : N = 2ⁿ, n ≥ 0}: which argument shows it is not regular?","options":["s = a^{2ᵖ}, i = 2: length 2ᵖ + k lies strictly between 2ᵖ and 2ᵖ⁺¹","s = aᵖ, i = 0","It is regular: (aa)*","s = a^{2ᵖ}, i = 0: length 0 is not a power of 2"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"1 ≤ k ≤ p < 2ᵖ, so 2ᵖ < 2ᵖ + k < 2ᵖ⁺¹. aᵖ is not in L for most p, and (aa)* is the even lengths.","source":"Tutorial 6 (CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx) Task 4(c)","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — Regular or not","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-025","chapter":"pump","topic":"Who chooses what","type":"mcq","q":"A student 'proves' a*b* is not regular with s = aᵖbᵖ, x = a^{p−1}, y = ab, z = b^{p−1}. What is wrong?","options":["Nothing — the proof is correct","Only that i = 2 should be i = 0","The student chose the split, and this split even has |xy| = p + 1 > p; a*b* is regular anyway","s is not in a*b*"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"The opponent picks the split. For every legal split y = aᵏ and every pumped string stays in a*b*, which is regular.","source":"Tutorial 6 (CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx) Task 5","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — Find the mistake","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-026","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"For L = {aᵐbⁿ : m ≤ n} with s = aᵖbᵖ, which i works?","options":["i = 0","i = 2","both 0 and 2","neither"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Pumping up adds a's: a^{p+k}bᵖ has m > n. Pumping down removes a's and keeps m ≤ n — the mirror image of {m > n}, where i = 0 works.","source":"Tutorial 6 (CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx) Task 1","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — Tutorial tasks","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-027","chapter":"pump","topic":"Statement","type":"mcq","q":"In the pumping game, which moves are YOURS?","options":["p and the split","s and i","p and i","the split and i"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Opponent: p and the split xyz. You: the string s (in terms of p) and the pump i. Slide 10: 'You pick s and i, never p or the split'.","source":"Tutorial 6 slides 7 and 10","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — The game","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-028","chapter":"pump","topic":"Statement","type":"tf","q":"If you cannot find a contradiction with the pumping lemma, the language is regular.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"Some non-regular languages pass the pumping test. To show regular, build a DFA/NFA/regex or use closure.","source":"Tutorial 6 (CS313_Tutorial_6_Pumping_Lemma.docx) §1.4 Keep in mind","study":"06-pumping-lemma.md — Keep in mind","origin":"professor"},{"id":"pump-029","chapter":"pump","topic":"Choosing s and i","type":"mcq","q":"Show {aⁿb^{kn} : n ≥ 0} is not regular for EVERY k ≥ 1. Which step is required?","options":["Prove it for k = 1 and k = 2 only","Fix an arbitrary k, use s = aᵖb^{kp}, i = 2; k(p + j) ≠ kp since j ≥ 1","Pick k = p","It is regular for large k"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"General-form 'show that' question (≈40% of the midterm pumping part): keep k a letter, never give it a value.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Question 5.1","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 5","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-001","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Sets","type":"tf","q":"{1} ∈ {1, 2}.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"The elements of {1,2} are the numbers 1 and 2, not the set {1}. But {1} ⊆ {1,2} and {1} ∈ P({1,2}).","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q1.1(b)","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-002","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Sets","type":"mcq","q":"P({∅}) = ?","options":["∅","{∅}","{∅, {∅}}","{{∅}}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"{∅} has one element, so 2¹ = 2 subsets: ∅ and {∅}.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q1.2","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-003","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Sets","type":"mcq","q":"A = {1,2,3}, B = {2,3,4}. |P(A × B)| = ?","options":["64","512","18","9"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"|A × B| = 9, so 2⁹ = 512.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q1.3","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-004","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Sets","type":"mcq","q":"40 students: 25 Java, 18 Python, 7 both. How many study neither?","options":["4","3","11","0"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"|J ∪ Y| = 25 + 18 − 7 = 36; 40 − 36 = 4.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q1.4","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-005","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Sets","type":"mcq","q":"A = {a, ab}, B = {ε, b}. How many strings are in AB?","options":["4","3","2","5"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"a, ab, ab, abb — ab appears twice and a set lists it once: {a, ab, abb}.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q1.8","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-006","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Sets","type":"mcq","q":"{x ∈ ℤ : x² < 10} = ?","options":["{0,1,2,3}","{1,2,3}","{−3,…,3}","{−3,−2,−1,1,2,3}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Negatives count: (−3)² = 9 < 10, and 0 is included.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q1.6(a)","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 1","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-007","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Proof techniques","type":"mcq","q":"'Let M′ be M with F replaced by Q − F; then M′ accepts w ⇔ w ∉ L.' Which technique proves 'complement of regular is regular'?","options":["Contradiction","Direct","Induction","Pigeonhole"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Assume L regular (P), reason forward to a DFA for Σ* − L (Q).","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx §2.1","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-008","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Proof techniques","type":"tf","q":"Swapping accept and non-accept states also complements the language of an NFA.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":true,"why":"An NFA can have one accepting and one rejecting path for the same string. Convert to a DFA first.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx §2.1","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-009","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Proof techniques","type":"mcq","q":"'For every n ≥ 1 there is a DFA with n states accepting a^k exactly when n | k' — proved by building the circle DFA. Which technique?","options":["Constructive","Nonconstructive","Contradiction","Direct only"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"You exhibit the object (states q₀…q_{n−1}, δ(qᵢ,a) = q_{(i+1) mod n}) and check it.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx §2.2","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-010","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Proof techniques","type":"mcq","q":"How does the proof that every DFA for {aᵏ : 3 | k} needs ≥ 3 states start?","options":["Base case k = 0","Suppose a DFA with at most 2 states recognizes L","Here is a 3-state DFA","Count all 2-state DFAs"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Proof by contradiction: assume the 2-state machine, force q₀ accept, q₁ reject with a self-loop, then aaa is rejected — impossible.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx §2.4","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-011","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Proof techniques","type":"mcq","q":"In that contradiction proof, which string breaks the 2-state DFA?","options":["a","aa","aaa","ε"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"ε forces q₀ accepting, a and aa force q₁ with a self-loop, so aaa ends in the rejecting q₁ although aaa ∈ L.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx §2.4","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 2","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-012","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Proof techniques","type":"mcq","q":"Which three techniques did the class vote to keep for the midterm?","options":["Direct, constructive, contradiction","Direct, induction, pigeonhole","Contradiction, induction, nonconstructive","All six"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Professor's message (09.11.2026): direct, constructive and contradiction.","source":"Professor's announcement 09.11.2026","study":"midterm-review.md — Scope","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-013","chapter":"mrev","topic":"DFA & NFA","type":"mcq","q":"DFA for 'contains aba': in state q₂ (input ends in ab), a b arrives. Next state?","options":["q₁","q₂","q₀","q₃"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"The input now ends in abb; no suffix of abb starts aba, so back to q₀.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Example 3.1","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-014","chapter":"mrev","topic":"DFA & NFA","type":"mcq","q":"The mod-3 DFA reads 10110 (= 22). Where does it end?","options":["r₀","r₁","r₂","trap"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Run r₀ r₁ r₂ r₂ r₂ r₁, and 22 mod 3 = 1.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q3.2(c)","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-015","chapter":"mrev","topic":"DFA & NFA","type":"mcq","q":"NFA N₅ (ε: 1→2; 1,2 –a→ 3; 2 –b→ 2; 3 –b→ 1). DFA start state?","options":["{1}","{1,2}","{2}","{1,2,3}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"q′ = C_ε({1}) = {1,2}: the NFA can move to 2 before reading anything.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Example 3.5","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-016","chapter":"mrev","topic":"DFA & NFA","type":"mcq","q":"In the N₅ conversion, δ′({3}, b) = ?","options":["{1}","{1,2}","∅","{2}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"3 –b→ 1, then take the closure C_ε({1}) = {1,2}. Forgetting this closure is the most common mistake.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Example 3.5","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-017","chapter":"mrev","topic":"DFA & NFA","type":"mcq","q":"The 4-state NFA for 'ends with 011' converts to a DFA with how many reachable states?","options":["16","8","4","5"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"{q₀}, {q₀,q₁}, {q₀,q₂}, {q₀,q₃} — far fewer than 2⁴.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q3.3","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 3","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-018","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Regular expressions","type":"mcq","q":"Regex for 'no two a's next to each other' over {a,b}?","options":["(b ∪ ab)*(ε ∪ a)","(a ∪ b)*b(a ∪ b)*","b*(ab)*","(ba)*"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Every a not at the end is followed by b; one final a may hang. Checked over all strings ≤ 11.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx §4.2","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 4","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-019","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Regular expressions","type":"mcq","q":"Describe a*(ba*ba*)*.","options":["Ends with b","Even number of b's","Even length","At most two b's"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Each block ba*ba* adds exactly two b's; a's go anywhere.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx §4.3","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 4","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-020","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Regular expressions","type":"mcq","q":"Which pair is NOT equal?","options":["(a ∪ b)* and (a*b*)*","a(ba)* and (ab)*a","a* ∪ b* and (a ∪ b)*","(a ∪ b)*a(a ∪ b)* and b*a(a ∪ b)*"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"ab ∈ (a ∪ b)* but ab ∉ a* ∪ b*. The other three pairs are equal.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Q4.2","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 4","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-021","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Regular expressions","type":"mcq","q":"State elimination on the mod-3 DFA gives which regex?","options":["(0 ∪ 1(01*0)*1)*","(0 ∪ 11)*","(01*0)*","0*(10*10*)*"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Remove r₂ → loop 01*0 on r₁; remove r₁ → loop 1(01*0)*1 on r₀; star the loop. Verified on all binary strings ≤ 12.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Example 4.3","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 4","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-022","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Regular expressions","type":"mcq","q":"DFA: 0 –a→ 1, 0 –b→ 3 (trap), 1 ↔ 2 on a,b, accept 2. Its language?","options":["Starts with a, odd length","Starts with a, even length","Contains a","Ends with a"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"After the first a, it is in 2 exactly when an odd number of symbols follow, so total length is even.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Example 4.1","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 4","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-023","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Pumping lemma","type":"mcq","q":"Proving the pumping lemma itself: why does a state repeat among r₀,…,r_p?","options":["Induction on |s|","p + 1 states visited, only p states exist — pigeonhole","The DFA is minimal","Because y ≠ ε"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"That repeat is the loop y, which gives |y| ≥ 1 and |xy| ≤ p.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx Question 5.2","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 5","origin":"professor"},{"id":"mrev-024","chapter":"mrev","topic":"Pumping lemma","type":"mcq","q":"How is the midterm's pumping part split?","options":["100% proofs of non-regularity","~60% worked examples, ~40% 'show that' in general form","Only stating the lemma","50% regex, 50% pumping"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Stated in the review's table: about 60% worked examples, about 40% 'show that' questions.","source":"CS313_Midterm_Review.docx table + §5","study":"midterm-review.md — Part 5","origin":"professor"},{"id":"l01-001","chapter":"l01","topic":"Why theory","type":"mcq","q":"Which of these is NOT one of the three fundamental questions the course sets out to answer?","options":["What can and what cannot be computed?","What is an algorithm?","Which programming language computes fastest?","When should an algorithm be considered practically feasible?"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 5 lists exactly three questions: computability, what an algorithm is, and practical feasibility. Language speed is an engineering question, not one of them.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 5","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Why theory (slides 5–8)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-002","chapter":"l01","topic":"Why theory","type":"mcq","q":"Why is the mathematics of Theory of Computation called \"discrete\"?","options":["Its emphasis is on finite sets and sequences","Its emphasis is on real numbers and continuous variables","It avoids sets and works only with numbers","It is only used to analyse hardware circuits"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 7: the emphasis is not on real numbers and continuous variables but on finite sets and sequences. The real-number option is the exact opposite of the slide.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 7","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Why theory (slides 5–8)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-003","chapter":"l01","topic":"Why theory","type":"tf","q":"According to slide 6, once a theory is widely accepted it is fixed and no longer open to change.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 6 lists \"dynamic (open to change)\" as a trait of a theory, alongside evidence-based, testable (confirmed or falsified) and predictive. Wide acceptance does not freeze it.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 6","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Why theory (slides 5–8)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-004","chapter":"l01","topic":"Membership","type":"mcq","q":"How many elements does A = {a, ∅, {a, ∅}} have?","options":["2","4","5","3"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"The elements are a, ∅ and the set {a, ∅}. The inner set counts as one element; do not unpack it (that would wrongly give 4 or 5).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 10","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Sets — basics (slides 9–12)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-005","chapter":"l01","topic":"Membership","type":"yn","q":"Let A = {(a, b), h}. Is (b, a) ∈ A?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Only the pair (a, b) and h are elements. Ordered pairs care about order, so (b, a) ≠ (a, b) and (b, a) ∉ A.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 11","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Sets — basics (slides 9–12)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-006","chapter":"l01","topic":"Membership","type":"mcq","q":"Let A = {(a, b), h}. Which of these is an element of A?","options":["a","(a, b)","b","∅"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 11: the pair (a, b) is an element, but its components a and b are not, and neither is ∅. Being inside a pair does not make something an element of the set.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 11","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Sets — basics (slides 9–12)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-007","chapter":"l01","topic":"Membership","type":"mcq","q":"In this course ℕ includes 0. What is {n ∈ ℕ : n < 3}?","options":["{1, 2}","{0, 1, 2}","{1, 2, 3}","{0, 1, 2, 3}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 12 gives {0, 1, 2}. 0 ∈ ℕ here, so {1, 2} misses an element, and 3 is excluded because the inequality is strict.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 12","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Sets — basics (slides 9–12)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-008","chapter":"l01","topic":"Membership","type":"tf","q":"{n ∈ ℕ : n < 0} = ∅","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"No natural number is negative, so no element satisfies the property and the set is empty (slide 12).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 12","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Sets — basics (slides 9–12)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-009","chapter":"l01","topic":"Membership","type":"yn","q":"Is ∅ ∈ {∅}?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"{∅} is a set with one element, and that element is ∅. So ∅ ∈ {∅}, while {∅} ≠ ∅ because ∅ has no elements at all.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 9–11","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Sets — basics (slides 9–12)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-010","chapter":"l01","topic":"Subsets & equality","type":"mcq","q":"A = {2, 3}, B = {1, 2, 3, 4}, C = {1, 3, 4, 5}. Which statement is true?","options":["A ⊆ C","B ⊆ A","C ⊆ B","A ⊆ B"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Every element of A (2 and 3) is in B. A ⊄ C because 2 ∉ C; B ⊄ A because 1 ∉ A; C ⊄ B because 5 ∉ B.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 16","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Subset, equality, disjointness (slides 15–18)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-011","chapter":"l01","topic":"Subsets & equality","type":"tf","q":"The empty set is an element of every set.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 17: ∅ is a subset of every set, but not necessarily an element of it. For example ∅ ∉ {1, 2}.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 17","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Subset, equality, disjointness (slides 15–18)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-012","chapter":"l01","topic":"Subsets & equality","type":"mcq","q":"To prove that two sets A and B are equal, what must you show?","options":["A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A","A ⊆ B only","A ∩ B ≠ ∅","A and B have the same number of elements"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 15 defines A = B as A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A, so both inclusions are needed. Equal size is not enough: {1} and {2} have the same size.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 15","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Subset, equality, disjointness (slides 15–18)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-013","chapter":"l01","topic":"Subsets & equality","type":"mcq","q":"What does A ⊂ B (proper subset) mean?","options":["A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A","A ∩ B = ∅","A ⊆ B and A ≠ B","A ⊆ B or A = B"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 15: proper subset = A ⊆ B and A ≠ B. \"A ⊆ B and B ⊆ A\" is the definition of equality, the opposite of proper.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 15","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Subset, equality, disjointness (slides 15–18)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-014","chapter":"l01","topic":"Subsets & equality","type":"yn","q":"Is {1, 2} ⊆ {{1, 2}, 3}?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"For ⊆, each element (1 and 2) would have to be in the right-hand set, but its elements are the set {1, 2} and the number 3. The correct statement is {1, 2} ∈ {{1, 2}, 3}.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 15","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Subset, equality, disjointness (slides 15–18)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-015","chapter":"l01","topic":"Set operations","type":"mcq","q":"Which pair of sets is disjoint?","options":["{1, 3, 9} and {3, 5, 7}","{∅} and {∅, 1}","{1, 3, 9} and {2, 4, 8}","{a} and {a, b}"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Disjoint means A ∩ B = ∅. {1,3,9} and {3,5,7} share 3, {∅} and {∅,1} share ∅, and {a} and {a,b} share a.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 18","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Subset, equality, disjointness (slides 15–18)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-016","chapter":"l01","topic":"Set operations","type":"mcq","q":"{1, 3, 9} ∪ {3, 5, 7} = ?","options":["{1, 3, 5, 7, 9}","{3}","{1, 9}","{1, 3, 3, 5, 7, 9}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Union takes every element in either set, each listed once. {3} is the intersection and {1, 9} the difference. Writing 3 twice is not how sets are written.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 19","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Set operations (slides 19, 25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-017","chapter":"l01","topic":"Set operations","type":"mcq","q":"{1, 3, 9} − {3, 5, 7} = ?","options":["{5, 7}","{1, 5, 7, 9}","{3}","{1, 9}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"A − B keeps the elements of A that are not in B, so 3 is removed and {1, 9} is left. {5, 7} is B − A, and {1, 5, 7, 9} is the symmetric difference.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 19","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Set operations (slides 19, 25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-018","chapter":"l01","topic":"Set operations","type":"mcq","q":"{1, 3, 9} ∩ {3, 5, 7} = ?","options":["∅","{3}","{1, 3, 5, 7, 9}","{1, 9}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"3 is the only element in both sets. ∅ would mean they are disjoint, and they are not.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 19","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Set operations (slides 19, 25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-019","chapter":"l01","topic":"Set operations","type":"mcq","q":"Universe U = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, A = {1, 2, 3}, B = {3, 4}. What is −(A ∪ B)?","options":["{6}","{5, 6}","{4, 5, 6}","{1, 2, 4, 5, 6}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"A ∪ B = {1, 2, 3, 4}, so its complement in U is {5, 6}. {4, 5, 6} is −A, which forgets that 4 ∈ B.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 19, 25","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Set operations (slides 19, 25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-020","chapter":"l01","topic":"Set operations","type":"tf","q":"The complement −A is defined only relative to a universe U with A ⊆ U, as −A = U − A.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 25: the complement is defined only for A ⊆ U, where U is the universe, and −A = U − A. Without a universe, \"everything not in A\" is not a set.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 25","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Set operations (slides 19, 25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-021","chapter":"l01","topic":"Power set","type":"mcq","q":"A = {1, a, 3}. How many elements does 𝒫(A) have?","options":["6","7","9","8"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"|𝒫(A)| = 2³ = 8 (slide 23 lists all eight). 6 forgets ∅ and A itself; 7 forgets one of them.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 22–23","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Power set (slides 21–23)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-022","chapter":"l01","topic":"Power set","type":"mcq","q":"Which is 𝒫({c, d})?","options":["{∅, {c}, {d}, {c, d}}","{{c}, {d}, {c, d}}","{∅, c, d, {c, d}}","{∅, {c}, {d}}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 21: the subsets are ∅, the singletons {c} and {d}, and {c, d} itself. c and d are elements, not subsets, so they cannot be members of the power set.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 21","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Power set (slides 21–23)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-023","chapter":"l01","topic":"Power set","type":"tf","q":"Professor's short question Q1. Is this true or false?\n𝒫({1, 2}) ∩ {1, 2} ≠ ∅","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"False: the intersection is ∅. Every element of 𝒫({1, 2}) = {∅, {1}, {2}, {1, 2}} is a set, while the elements of {1, 2} are the numbers 1 and 2, so nothing is in both (slide 34).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 32–34","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Q&A slides (31–36, figures)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"l01-024","chapter":"l01","topic":"Power set","type":"tf","q":"Professor's short question Q2. Is this true or false?\n{{a, b}} ∈ 𝒫({a, b, {a, b}})","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"True (slide 33). {a, b} is an element of S = {a, b, {a, b}}, so {{a, b}} ⊆ S and therefore {{a, b}} ∈ 𝒫(S). Slide 35 cites {a, b} ⊆ S, but that only shows {a, b} ∈ 𝒫(S); the fact that decides Q2 is {a, b} ∈ S.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 32–35","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Q&A slides (31–36, figures)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"l01-025","chapter":"l01","topic":"Power set","type":"tf","q":"Professor's short question Q3. Is this true or false?\n∅ ∈ 𝒫({a, b, {a, b}})","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"True (slide 36). ∅ ⊆ A for every set A, so ∅ is always an element of the power set.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 32–33, 36","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Q&A slides (31–36, figures)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"l01-026","chapter":"l01","topic":"Power set","type":"mcq","q":"How many elements does 𝒫({a, b, {a, b}}) have?","options":["4","6","8","16"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"{a, b, {a, b}} has 3 distinct elements, since {a, b} is a different object from a and from b. So the power set has 2³ = 8. 4 comes from wrongly treating {a, b} as a duplicate.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 21, 32","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Power set (slides 21–23)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-027","chapter":"l01","topic":"Power set","type":"mcq","q":"|𝒫(𝒫(∅))| = ?","options":["0","1","2","4"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"𝒫(∅) = {∅} has 1 element, so 𝒫(𝒫(∅)) = {∅, {∅}} has 2¹ = 2. The power set is never empty: it always contains ∅.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 21","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Power set (slides 21–23)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-028","chapter":"l01","topic":"Cartesian products","type":"mcq","q":"When are the ordered pairs (a, b) and (c, d) equal?","options":["When a = c and b = d","When {a, b} = {c, d}","When a = d and b = c","When a = c or b = d"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 26 says (a, b) = (c, d) iff a = c and b = d. Equality as sets ignores order, so (1, 2) and (2, 1) would wrongly count as equal.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 26, 30","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Ordered pairs & Cartesian product (slides 26–30)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-029","chapter":"l01","topic":"Cartesian products","type":"mcq","q":"A = {3, 1}. How many ordered triples are in A × A × A?","options":["6","9","3","8"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"|Aⁿ| = |A|ⁿ = 2³ = 8 (slide 29). 6 = 2·3 multiplies instead of taking a power.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 29","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Ordered pairs & Cartesian product (slides 26–30)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-030","chapter":"l01","topic":"Cartesian products","type":"tf","q":"A = {3, 1}. (3, 1, 1) ∈ A × A × A.\n(Slide 29's final list omits this triple and lists (1, 1, 1) twice. The question asks about the corrected set.)","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Every triple with entries from {3, 1} is in A³. The slide itself lists (3, 1, 1) under \"first coordinate = 3\", and the final list's duplicate (1, 1, 1) is a typo.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 29","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Ordered pairs & Cartesian product (slides 26–30)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-031","chapter":"l01","topic":"Cartesian products","type":"mcq","q":"How many ordered pairs are in {3, 1, 9} × {b, c, d}?","options":["6","9","12","27"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"|A × B| = |A|·|B| = 3·3 = 9 (slide 30 lists them). 6 adds the sizes and 27 cubes them.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 30","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Ordered pairs & Cartesian product (slides 26–30)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-032","chapter":"l01","topic":"Cartesian products","type":"mcq","q":"{1, 2} × {x} = ?","options":["{(x, 1), (x, 2)}","{(1, x), (2, x)}","{1, 2, x}","{(1, x), (2, x), (x, 1), (x, 2)}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"A × B pairs first components from A with second components from B. {(x, 1), (x, 2)} is {x} × {1, 2}, and {1, 2, x} is the union.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 26–28","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Ordered pairs & Cartesian product (slides 26–30)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-033","chapter":"l01","topic":"Partitions & big unions","type":"mcq","q":"Which is a partition of {a, b, c, d}?","options":["{{b, c}, {c, d}}","{{a, b}, {c}, {d}, ∅}","{{a}, {b, c}}","{{a, b}, {c}, {d}}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 24 requires non-empty blocks that are pairwise disjoint and whose union is the whole set. {{b,c},{c,d}} overlaps on c and misses a, the version with ∅ has an empty block, and {{a},{b,c}} misses d.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 24","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Set operations (slides 19, 25) — Partition","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l01-034","chapter":"l01","topic":"Partitions & big unions","type":"mcq","q":"S = {{a, b}, {b, c}, {c, d}}. What is ⋃S?","options":["{a, b, c, d}","{b, c}","∅","{{a, b}, {b, c}, {c, d}}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"⋃S collects the elements of all the member sets: {a, b, c, d} (slide 20's own example). ∅ is ⋂S, since no element is in all three sets.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 1 slide 20","study":"01-sets-and-operations.md — Set operations (slides 19, 25) — Big union","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-001","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relations as sets","type":"mcq","q":"In set theory, what is a binary relation on sets A and B?","options":["A single ordered pair (a, b)","Any subset of A ∪ B","Any subset of A × B","A function from A to B"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 4: any R ⊆ A × B is a binary relation. Slide 3's \"R = (a, b)\" is an erratum: a single pair is an element of R, not R itself. Every function is a relation, but most relations are not functions.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 2–4","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Binary relations, domain and range (slides 4–5)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-002","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relations as sets","type":"mcq","q":"Why must the elements of a relation such as \"less than\" be ordered pairs rather than two-element sets?","options":["Because {4, 7} = {7, 4}, so a set cannot record which number is smaller","Because a set cannot contain two numbers at the same time","Because every relation must be a finite set of elements","Because ordered pairs are the only objects a set may contain"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 3: an unordered set {4, 7} is the same as {7, 4}, but 4 < 7 holds and 7 < 4 does not. Only the ordered pair (4, 7) can tell them apart.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 3","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Relations as sets (slides 2–3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-003","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relations as sets","type":"tf","q":"Slide 3 writes \"R = (a, b)\". As corrected in the errata: a relation R is a single ordered pair.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"A relation is a SET of ordered pairs, R ⊆ A × B. (a, b) is one element of R. The slide's notation is an erratum.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 3–4","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Relations as sets (slides 2–3)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-004","chapter":"l02","topic":"Domain & range","type":"mcq","q":"R = {(a, 3), (c, 2), (b, 5), (d, 3)}. What is the range of R?","options":["{a, b, c, d}","{3}","{2, 5}","{2, 3, 5}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"The range is the set of second components actually used: 3, 2, 5, with the repeated 3 written once (slide 5). {a, b, c, d} is the domain.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 5","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Binary relations, domain and range (slides 4–5)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-005","chapter":"l02","topic":"Domain & range","type":"mcq","q":"R ⊆ {1, 2, 3, 4} × {x, y} with R = {(1, x), (3, x), (3, y)}. What is the domain of R?","options":["{1, 2, 3, 4}","{1, 3}","{x, y}","{1}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The domain is {a : ∃b with (a, b) ∈ R}, the first components actually used, which here is {1, 3}. {1, 2, 3, 4} is the whole set A, and {x, y} is the range.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 4","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Binary relations, domain and range (slides 4–5)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-006","chapter":"l02","topic":"Domain & range","type":"mcq","q":"Professor's in-class exercise: f = {(a, 3), (c, 2), (b, 5), (d, 3)}. What is the domain of f?","options":["{2, 3, 5}","{a, b, c, d}","{a, c, b, d, 3}","{a, d}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The domain is the set of first components: {a, c, b, d} = {a, b, c, d} (slide 31). {2, 3, 5} is the range.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 30–31","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — In-class exercise (slides 30–31)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"l02-007","chapter":"l02","topic":"Domain & range","type":"mcq","q":"For a function f : A → B, which is always true?","options":["The range always equals the codomain, whatever f is declared as","The codomain can always be read off the list of pairs of f","The codomain is a subset of the range, and equal when f is injective","The range is a subset of the codomain, and equal to it exactly when f is surjective"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"The codomain B is declared in f : A → B. The range is the set of values actually hit, so range ⊆ B. This is why slide 31's \"codomain = {3, 2, 5, 3}\" is an erratum.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 29, 31","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — In-class exercise (slides 30–31)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-008","chapter":"l02","topic":"Domain & range","type":"mcq","q":"If R ⊆ A₁ × A₂ × ⋯ × Aₙ and all the Aᵢ are the same set A, what is R called?","options":["An n-ary relation on A","A binary relation on A","A function on A","An equivalence relation on A"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 6: when A₁ = ⋯ = Aₙ = A, R ⊆ Aⁿ is an n-ary relation defined in the set A. It is binary only when n = 2.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 6","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — n-ary relations (slide 6)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-009","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"mcq","q":"Professor's question, slide 10 diagram B: R = {(a, b), (b, a), (a, d), (d, c), (c, c), (c, a)} on {a, b, c, d}. Only c has a self-loop. Which describes R?","options":["Reflexive","Irreflexive","Neither reflexive nor irreflexive","Both reflexive and irreflexive"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Reflexive needs a loop on every node, but (a,a), (b,b) and (d,d) are missing. Irreflexive needs no loops at all, but (c,c) is present. So R is neither (slide 10).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 8, 10","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"l02-010","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"yn","q":"Same relation R = {(a, b), (b, a), (a, d), (d, c), (c, c), (c, a)}. Is R symmetric?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"(a, d) ∈ R but (d, a) ∉ R, so symmetry fails. The two-way pair a ↔ b alone does not make the whole relation symmetric.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 8, 12","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-011","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"mcq","q":"R = {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (1,2), (2,1)} on {1, 2, 3}. Which describes R?","options":["Reflexive and symmetric, but not transitive","Symmetric and transitive, but not reflexive","An equivalence relation","Reflexive and transitive, but not symmetric"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Every node has a loop, every arrow has its reverse, and each 2-step path (1→2→1, 2→1→2) has its shortcut (1,1) and (2,2). So R is an equivalence relation with classes {1, 2} and {3}.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 9–17","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Equivalence relations and classes (slides 17–19)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-012","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"yn","q":"R = {(1, 2), (2, 3), (1, 3)} on {1, 2, 3}. Is R transitive?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The only two-step path is 1 → 2 → 3, and its shortcut (1, 3) is in R. Without (1, 3), R would not be transitive.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 16","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-013","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"mcq","q":"R = {(1,1), (2,2), (3,3), (1,2), (2,3), (1,3)} on {1, 2, 3} (this is ≤). Which property does R NOT have?","options":["Symmetric","Reflexive","Transitive","Antisymmetric"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(1, 2) ∈ R but (2, 1) ∉ R, so R is not symmetric. It is reflexive (all loops), transitive (1→2→3 has 1→3) and antisymmetric (no two-way arrow between distinct nodes).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 9–16","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-014","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"yn","q":"R = {(1, 1), (2, 2), (1, 2), (2, 1)} on {1, 2, 3}. Is R symmetric and transitive?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Every arrow has its reverse, and every 2-step path has its shortcut. It is NOT reflexive on {1, 2, 3} because (3, 3) is missing, which is why reflexivity has to be checked separately.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 9–16","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-015","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"mcq","q":"Professor's question (slides 14–15): why is \"a is the brother of b\" neither symmetric nor antisymmetric?","options":["Nobody is their own brother, so the relation fails to be reflexive","Brothers have the same parents, so it is really an equivalence relation","It is not transitive, and a relation that is not transitive cannot be symmetric","b may be a's sister, so (b, a) can fail; yet two brothers relate in both directions"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Symmetry fails because if b is a's sister, a is her brother but she is not his brother. Antisymmetry fails because two brothers are each other's brother. Reflexivity and transitivity have nothing to do with the question.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 14–15","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"l02-016","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"mcq","q":"On the set of all persons, \"a is the father of b\" is the professor's example of which property?","options":["Symmetric","Antisymmetric","Transitive","Reflexive"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 13: if a is b's father and a ≠ b, then b cannot be a's father. Spouse is the symmetric example and ancestor is the transitive one. Father is not transitive: a grandfather is not a father.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 12–13, 16","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-017","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"tf","q":"A relation can be neither reflexive nor irreflexive.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Not reflexive means at least one loop is missing. Irreflexive means every loop is missing. Slide 10's diagram B is neither, so the two are not opposites.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 10","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-018","chapter":"l02","topic":"Relation properties","type":"tf","q":"Corrected version of the slide 9 note: reflexivity requires that every arrow between two distinct nodes has a partner going back.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"That property is symmetry. Reflexivity only demands a self-loop on every node and says nothing about pairs of distinct nodes. Slide 9's NOTE is an erratum.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 9, 12","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — The four properties (slides 9–16)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-019","chapter":"l02","topic":"Equivalence relations","type":"mcq","q":"Which three properties together make an equivalence relation?","options":["Reflexive, antisymmetric, transitive","Reflexive, symmetric, transitive","Symmetric, antisymmetric, transitive","Irreflexive, symmetric, transitive"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 17. Reflexive + ANTIsymmetric + transitive is a partial order such as ≤, not an equivalence.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 17","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Equivalence relations and classes (slides 17–19)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-020","chapter":"l02","topic":"Equivalence relations","type":"mcq","q":"On {0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, let a ∼ b iff a and b leave the same remainder when divided by 3. What is [1]?","options":["{1}","{0, 3}","{1, 2, 4, 5}","{1, 4}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"[1] = {b : b ∼ 1} = the numbers with remainder 1, so {1, 4}. {0, 3} is [0]. A class always contains its own representative.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 19","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Equivalence relations and classes (slides 17–19)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-021","chapter":"l02","topic":"Equivalence relations","type":"mcq","q":"Take the strings ε, a, b, aa, ab, ba, bb with s ∼ t iff |s| = |t| (slide 18's relation). How many equivalence classes are there?","options":["3","2","4","7"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"One class per length: {ε}, {a, b}, {aa, ab, ba, bb}, which is 3 classes. 7 counts strings, not classes, and 2 forgets the empty string ε.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 18–19","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Equivalence relations and classes (slides 17–19)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-022","chapter":"l02","topic":"Equivalence relations","type":"mcq","q":"a ∼ b iff persons a and b have the same parents. What are the equivalence classes?","options":["Pairs of parents","Single persons","Groups of siblings","Whole families including the parents"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 19: people with the same parents cluster into sibling groups. The parents themselves are not related to their children by this relation.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 19","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Equivalence relations and classes (slides 17–19)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-023","chapter":"l02","topic":"Equivalence relations","type":"tf","q":"The equivalence classes of an equivalence relation on a set A form a partition of A.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The classes are non-empty (a ∈ [a]), pairwise disjoint, and their union is A. That is exactly Lecture 1's definition of a partition (slide 24).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 19 · Lecture 1 slide 24","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Equivalence relations and classes (slides 17–19)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-024","chapter":"l02","topic":"Equivalence relations","type":"yn","q":"On ℤ, let a ∼ b iff a − b is a multiple of 3. Is ∼ an equivalence relation?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Reflexive: a − a = 0 is a multiple of 3. Symmetric: if a − b is a multiple of 3, so is b − a. Transitive: a − c = (a − b) + (b − c), a sum of two multiples of 3. Compare \"<\", which is transitive but neither reflexive nor symmetric.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 17 · cf. Sipser Example 0.11 (same argument for ≡₇)","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Equivalence relations and classes (slides 17–19)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-025","chapter":"l02","topic":"Cardinality & countability","type":"mcq","q":"According to the course definition, when is a set A finite?","options":["When there is n ∈ ℕ and an injection f : ℕ → A from all of ℕ","When A is a proper subset of ℕ = {0, 1, 2, …}","When there is n ∈ ℕ and a bijection f : {0, 1, …, n−1} → A","When A is countable, i.e. equinumerous with a subset of ℕ"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 20. A proper subset of ℕ such as the evens can be infinite, and countable sets include infinite ones like ℕ.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 20–21","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-026","chapter":"l02","topic":"Cardinality & countability","type":"mcq","q":"What does \"countable\" mean?","options":["Finite or countably infinite","Finite only","Infinite only","Having fewer elements than ℕ"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 21: a set is countable if it is finite or if it is countably infinite (equinumerous with ℕ). ℕ itself is countable.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 21, 23","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-027","chapter":"l02","topic":"Cardinality & countability","type":"tf","q":"Slide 21 gives {1, 2, …, n} as an example. As corrected: {1, 2, …, n} for a natural number n is countably infinite.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"{1, 2, …, n} has n elements, so it is FINITE. Correct examples of countably infinite sets are ℕ, the evens, or ℤ. This is a slide 21 erratum.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 21","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-028","chapter":"l02","topic":"Cardinality & countability","type":"mcq","q":"For infinite sets, what does it mean for two sets to have \"the same size\"?","options":["One is a subset of the other","They have the same number of elements written out","They are both subsets of ℕ","A bijection exists between them"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 23: for infinite sets same size is not a count but the existence of a bijection. The evens are a proper subset of ℕ yet have the same size.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 23","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-029","chapter":"l02","topic":"Cardinality & countability","type":"mcq","q":"With ℕ = {0, 1, 2, …} (0 ∈ ℕ in this course), which map is a bijection from ℕ onto B = {2, 4, 6, 8, …}?","options":["f(n) = 2n","f(n) = 2n + 2","f(n) = n + 2","f(n) = n²"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"f(n) = 2n + 2 sends 0, 1, 2, … to 2, 4, 6, … one-to-one and onto. Slide 23's f(n) = 2n works only if ℕ starts at 1; with 0 ∈ ℕ it hits 0 ∉ B. Either way, ℕ and the evens are equinumerous.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 23 · Lecture 1 slide 12","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-030","chapter":"l02","topic":"Cardinality & countability","type":"tf","q":"Equinumerosity is symmetric: if there is a bijection f : A → B, there is also a bijection from B to A.","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 24: the inverse f⁻¹ : B → A of a bijection is itself a bijection.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 24","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-031","chapter":"l02","topic":"Functions","type":"mcq","q":"A = {1, 2, 3}, B = {x, y}. Which relation is a function from A to B?","options":["{(1, x), (2, y)}","{(1, x), (2, x), (3, y)}","{(1, x), (1, y), (2, x), (3, y)}","{(x, 1), (y, 2), (x, 3)}"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 27: each a ∈ A must have EXACTLY one pair. The second option leaves 3 without an output, the third gives 1 two outputs, and the fourth goes from B to A. Two inputs sharing x is allowed.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 27","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Functions (slides 27–32)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-032","chapter":"l02","topic":"Functions","type":"mcq","q":"C = US cities, S = US states. Why is g = {(x, y) : x ∈ S, y ∈ C, x is the state for city y} NOT a function?","options":["Some cities lie in no state, so some inputs have no output","Some states have no cities, so some outputs are never used","Cities and states are different kinds of objects, so they cannot be paired","One state contains many cities, so one input has several outputs"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 28: f (city ↦ its state) is a function because each city is in exactly one state. Reversed, a state is paired with many cities, which breaks \"exactly one\".","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 28","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Functions (slides 27–32)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-033","chapter":"l02","topic":"Functions","type":"mcq","q":"f = {(a, 3), (c, 2), (b, 5), (d, 3)} and f(a) = 3. In slide 32's terms, what is 3?","options":["The image of a under f","The argument of f","The domain of f","The codomain of f"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 32: f(a) = 3 is the image of a under f. a is the argument and 3 the corresponding value, just like a function call returning a result.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 32","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Functions (slides 27–32)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-034","chapter":"l02","topic":"Injective & surjective","type":"mcq","q":"f : {1, 2, 3} → {a, b, c, d} with f(1) = a, f(2) = b, f(3) = c. Which describes f?","options":["Surjective but not injective","Bijective","Injective but not surjective","Neither injective nor surjective"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"No two inputs share an output, so f is injective. d is never hit, so f is not surjective. A bijection {1,2,3} → {a,b,c,d} is impossible anyway because the sizes differ.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 20","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-035","chapter":"l02","topic":"Injective & surjective","type":"mcq","q":"f : ℕ → ℕ, f(n) = n + 1, with 0 ∈ ℕ. Which describes f?","options":["Surjective but not injective","Bijective","Injective but not surjective","Neither injective nor surjective"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Distinct inputs give distinct outputs, so f is injective. But 0 is never an output, so f is not onto ℕ. Forgetting 0 ∈ ℕ makes it look bijective.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 20, 25","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-036","chapter":"l02","topic":"Injective & surjective","type":"yn","q":"f = {(a, 3), (c, 2), (b, 5), (d, 3)}. Does f have an inverse function f⁻¹ from its range back to {a, b, c, d}?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"f(a) = f(d) = 3, so f is not injective. The reversed relation would send 3 to two different values, so it is not a function. Only bijections have inverses (slide 24).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 24, 27, 30","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-037","chapter":"l02","topic":"Injective & surjective","type":"mcq","q":"f : {1, 2, 3} → {a, b, c} is the bijection f(1) = b, f(2) = c, f(3) = a. What is f⁻¹(a)?","options":["3","1","2","b"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"f⁻¹(a) is the argument whose image is a. Since f(3) = a, f⁻¹(a) = 3. \"1\" reads f in the wrong direction (a is the first letter).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 24, 32","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Finite sets, cardinality, countability (slides 20–25)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l02-038","chapter":"l02","topic":"Functions","type":"mcq","q":"f = {(1, a), (2, b), (3, a)} and g = {(a, x), (b, y)}. What is g(f(3))?","options":["y","a","3","x"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Apply f first: f(3) = a. Then g(a) = x. The answer a stops after the inner function. (Composition is not on the slides; this just applies slide 32's f(a) notation twice.)","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 27, 32","study":"02-relations-and-functions.md — Functions (slides 27–32)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-001","chapter":"l03","topic":"Theorems & proofs","type":"mcq","q":"Per slide 33, which of these may NOT be used as a statement in a proof?","options":["Axioms, the basic rules taken as given","The hypotheses of the theorem being proved","The theorem's conclusion, assumed without proof","Theorems that were already proved earlier"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 33 allows axioms, the theorem's hypotheses and previously proved theorems. Assuming the conclusion is circular reasoning.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 33","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Theorem, proof, axiom, hypothesis (slide 33)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-002","chapter":"l03","topic":"Theorems & proofs","type":"mcq","q":"What is an axiom?","options":["A fundamental statement accepted as true without proof, used as a starting point","A statement proved true from earlier theorems, then reused as a lemma","A proposed explanation that still requires further testing and confirmation","A statement shown to be false by exhibiting one counterexample"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 33 footnote 1. \"A proposed explanation that still needs testing\" is the footnote's definition of a hypothesis, the usual mix-up.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 33","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Theorem, proof, axiom, hypothesis (slide 33)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-003","chapter":"l03","topic":"Direct & deductive proofs","type":"mcq","q":"In the direct proof that n odd ⟹ n² odd, n = 2k + 1. Which rewriting finishes the proof?","options":["n² = 2(2k² + 1)","n² = 4k² + 1","n² = (2k)² + 2","n² = 2(2k² + 2k) + 1"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"(2k+1)² = 4k² + 4k + 1 = 2(2k² + 2k) + 1, which is even plus one and so odd. 4k² + 1 drops the 4k cross term.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 36","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 1. Direct proofs (slides 36–37)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-004","chapter":"l03","topic":"Direct & deductive proofs","type":"mcq","q":"A graph has 5 vertices and 7 edges. By the Handshaking Lemma, what is the sum of all vertex degrees?","options":["7","14","12","35"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Σ deg(v) = 2|E| = 2·7 = 14, because each edge has two ends. The number of vertices does not matter.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 37","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 1. Direct proofs (slides 36–37) — Example B","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-005","chapter":"l03","topic":"Direct & deductive proofs","type":"yn","q":"Can a graph have four vertices with degrees 1, 2, 2, 2?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The degrees sum to 7, which is odd. The Handshaking Lemma says the sum is 2|E|, which is always even.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 37","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 1. Direct proofs (slides 36–37) — Example B","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-006","chapter":"l03","topic":"Direct & deductive proofs","type":"mcq","q":"Professor's question (slides 57–58): which proof technique was used to prove the first De Morgan law?","options":["Proof by contradiction","Proof by deduction (a direct proof)","Proof by induction","Nonconstructive proof"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 58: proof by deduction, aka direct proof. Each line follows from the definitions of −, ∪ and ∩, with no assumed negation and no induction.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 57–58","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Proving De Morgan's first law (slides 54–56)","origin":"professor"},{"id":"l03-007","chapter":"l03","topic":"Direct & deductive proofs","type":"mcq","q":"Proving \"if A ⊆ B and B ⊆ C then A ⊆ C\" by deduction (slide 52). What is the first step?","options":["Assume A ⊄ C","Check the case A = ∅","Let x ∈ C","Let x ∈ A"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"To show A ⊆ C, take an arbitrary x ∈ A and push it through A ⊆ B and then B ⊆ C. Assuming A ⊄ C would turn it into a contradiction proof, and starting from x ∈ C goes the wrong way.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 52","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 7. Proof by deduction (slides 51–52)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-008","chapter":"l03","topic":"Constructive vs nonconstructive","type":"mcq","q":"Applying slide 38's construction r = (a + b)/2 to a = 1/3 and b = 1/2, which rational does it produce?","options":["5/12","5/6","1/6","1/12"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"(1/3 + 1/2)/2 = (5/6)/2 = 5/12, and 1/3 < 5/12 < 1/2. 5/6 forgets to halve, and 1/6 is b − a.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 38–40","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 2. Constructive proofs (slides 38–40)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-009","chapter":"l03","topic":"Constructive vs nonconstructive","type":"mcq","q":"Why is the proof that irrational x, y exist with xʸ rational (using √2^√2) called nonconstructive?","options":["It assumes the negation and derives a contradiction from it","It relies on a computer calculation of √2^√2 to many digits","It shows one of two pairs works but never says which","It only works for √2 and cannot be applied to other numbers"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Either √2^√2 is rational (take x = y = √2) or it is not (take x = √2^√2, y = √2). One case must hold, but the proof never decides which, so no specific pair is exhibited.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 41","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 3. Nonconstructive proofs (slides 41–42)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-010","chapter":"l03","topic":"Constructive vs nonconstructive","type":"mcq","q":"In the corrected √2^√2 proof (slide 41 is garbled), Case 2 assumes √2^√2 is irrational and sets x = √2^√2, y = √2. What is xʸ?","options":["√2","√2^√2","2","4"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"(√2^√2)^√2 = √2^(√2·√2) = √2² = 2, which is rational. That finishes Case 2.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 41","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 3. Nonconstructive proofs (slides 41–42)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-011","chapter":"l03","topic":"Constructive vs nonconstructive","type":"mcq","q":"Hair counts run from 0 to 1 000 000. What is the smallest city population that guarantees two people with the same hair count?","options":["1 000 002","1 000 000","1 000 001","500 001"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Counts 0 to 1 000 000 give 1 000 001 holes, so 1 000 002 pigeons are needed (slide 42, with its typo 1,000,0000 corrected). 1 000 001 people could all differ.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 42","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 3. Nonconstructive proofs (slides 41–42)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-012","chapter":"l03","topic":"Pigeonhole principle","type":"mcq","q":"How does slide 43 state the pigeon-hole principle formally?","options":["If |A| > |B|, there is no onto function from A to B","If |A| < |B|, there is no one-to-one function from A to B","If |A| = |B|, every function from A to B is a bijection","If |A| > |B|, there is no one-to-one function from A to B"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"With more objects than boxes, an injection would need a private box for each object, which is impossible. Onto functions from a bigger set to a smaller one can exist.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 43","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 4. The pigeon-hole principle (slides 43–44)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-013","chapter":"l03","topic":"Pigeonhole principle","type":"mcq","q":"In slide 44's proof that among 5 distinct integers two leave the same remainder mod 4, what are the pigeons and the holes?","options":["Pigeons = the 4 remainders; holes = the 5 integers","Pigeons = the 5 integers; holes = the 4 remainders 0, 1, 2, 3","Pigeons = the integers 0–3; holes = the 5 integers","Pigeons = pairs of integers; holes = remainders"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Pigeons are what you have more of (5 integers). Holes are the possible outcomes (4 remainders). 5 > 4 forces a shared hole.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 44","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 4. The pigeon-hole principle (slides 43–44)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-014","chapter":"l03","topic":"Pigeonhole principle","type":"mcq","q":"What is the minimum number of integers that guarantees two of them leave the same remainder when divided by 7?","options":["7","8","6","14"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"There are 7 remainders (0–6), so 7 + 1 = 8 pigeons force a collision. With 7 integers they could all have different remainders.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 43–44","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 4. The pigeon-hole principle (slides 43–44)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-015","chapter":"l03","topic":"Pigeonhole principle","type":"yn","q":"Does any group of 13 people guarantee that two share a birth month?","options":["Yes","No"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"13 people (pigeons) and 12 months (holes): 13 > 12 forces a shared month. With 12 people there is no guarantee.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 43","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 4. The pigeon-hole principle (slides 43–44)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-016","chapter":"l03","topic":"Proof by contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"What principle makes proof by contradiction valid?","options":["Showing S ⟹ true is enough to prove S","Checking S on enough examples proves S","Proving S for n = 1 and n ⟹ n+1","Showing ¬S ⟹ false is enough to prove S"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 45: if assuming ¬S leads to something false, ¬S cannot hold, so S is true. The last option describes induction.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 45","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 5. Proof by contradiction (slides 45–47)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-017","chapter":"l03","topic":"Proof by contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"To prove √2 is irrational by contradiction, what do you assume first?","options":["√2 = m/n for integers m, n with no common factor (lowest terms)","√2 is irrational, so it cannot be written as any fraction m/n","m and n are both even integers, so m/n can be reduced","√2 is not a real number, so it has no decimal expansion"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"You assume the negation, that √2 is rational, written in lowest terms. \"m and n both even\" is the conclusion that produces the contradiction, not the assumption.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 47","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 5. Proof by contradiction (slides 45–47) — Example B","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-018","chapter":"l03","topic":"Proof by contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"In the √2 proof, m² = 2n² gives m even, so m = 2k. Substituting gives which equation?","options":["m² = 4k","n = 2k","n² = 2k²","n² = 4k²"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"(2k)² = 2n² ⟹ 4k² = 2n² ⟹ n² = 2k². So n² is even and n is even, which contradicts lowest terms.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 47","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 5. Proof by contradiction (slides 45–47) — Example B","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-019","chapter":"l03","topic":"Proof by contradiction","type":"mcq","q":"Slide 46 proves \"if n² is even then n is even\" by assuming n² even and n odd. Which earlier result produces the contradiction?","options":["The Handshaking Lemma (degree sum = 2|E|)","The pigeon-hole principle with 2 holes","n odd ⟹ n² odd (the slide 36 direct proof)","√2 is irrational (the slide 47 proof)"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"If n is odd then n² is odd, but n² was assumed even. Reusing a proved theorem is allowed by slide 33. The √2 proof depends on this result, not the other way round.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 36, 46","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 5. Proof by contradiction (slides 45–47) — Example A","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-020","chapter":"l03","topic":"Induction","type":"mcq","q":"Proving P(n): 1 + 2 + ⋯ + n = n(n+1)/2 by induction, what is the base case? (Slide 49 mislabels its steps and never checks it.)","options":["n = 1: the left side is 1 and 1·2/2 = 1","Assume 1 + ⋯ + k = k(k+1)/2","Show 1 + ⋯ + (k+1) = (k+1)(k+2)/2","The statement P(n) itself"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"The base case verifies P at the starting value. The second option is the induction hypothesis and the third is the step. Slide 49 prints the theorem under \"base case\", which is an erratum.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 48–49","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 6. Proof by induction (slides 48–50)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-021","chapter":"l03","topic":"Induction","type":"mcq","q":"In the inductive step for 1 + ⋯ + n = n(n+1)/2, you rewrite k(k+1)/2 + (k+1). What must it equal?","options":["k(k+2)/2","(k+1)²/2","k(k+1)/2 + 1","(k+1)(k+2)/2"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"[k(k+1) + 2(k+1)]/2 = (k+1)(k+2)/2, which is the formula with n = k+1. That is exactly what P(k+1) claims.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 49","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 6. Proof by induction (slides 48–50)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-022","chapter":"l03","topic":"Induction","type":"mcq","q":"In slide 50's induction on the number of edges m (Handshaking Lemma), what is the base case?","options":["m = 1: a single edge gives degree sum 1 + 1 = 2","m = 0: no edges, so every degree is 0 and the sum is 0 = 2·0","Remove an edge {a, b} to get G′, which has m edges","Assume the degree sum is 2m for every graph with m edges"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"The base case is m = 0. Removing an edge is the inductive step, and \"assume 2m\" is the hypothesis. Slide 50 labels the m = 0 case \"Induction hypothesis\", which is an erratum.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 50","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 6. Proof by induction (slides 48–50)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-023","chapter":"l03","topic":"Induction","type":"tf","q":"An induction proof is valid even without a base case, as long as the step P(k) ⟹ P(k+1) is proved.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"Without a base case nothing starts the chain. For example, P(n): \"n = n + 1\" satisfies P(k) ⟹ P(k+1), yet it is false for every n.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 48–50","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 6. Proof by induction (slides 48–50)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-024","chapter":"l03","topic":"Induction","type":"mcq","q":"Using the formula proved by induction, what is 1 + 2 + ⋯ + 100?","options":["5000","5050","10100","4950"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"n(n+1)/2 = 100·101/2 = 5050. 10100 forgets to divide by 2, and 4950 is 99·100/2 (off by one).","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 49","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — 6. Proof by induction (slides 48–50)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-025","chapter":"l03","topic":"Set laws & De Morgan","type":"mcq","q":"A = {1, 2, 3, 4}, B = {2, 5}, C = {3, 6}. What is A − (B ∪ C)?","options":["{1, 3, 4}","{1, 2, 4}","{2, 3}","{1, 4}"],"answer":3,"fixed":false,"why":"B ∪ C = {2, 3, 5, 6}, and removing it from A leaves {1, 4} (slide 56). {1, 3, 4} is only A − B, and {1, 2, 4} is only A − C.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 56","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Proving De Morgan's first law (slides 54–56)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-026","chapter":"l03","topic":"Set laws & De Morgan","type":"mcq","q":"A = {1, 2, 3, 4}, B = {2, 3, 5}, C = {3, 4}. What is A − (B ∩ C)?","options":["{1, 2, 4}","{1}","{1, 4}","{3}"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"B ∩ C = {3}, so A − {3} = {1, 2, 4}. Equivalently, (A − B) ∪ (A − C) = {1, 4} ∪ {1, 2} = {1, 2, 4}. {1} is A − (B ∪ C), the other law.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 53, 59","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Set theory laws (slide 53)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-027","chapter":"l03","topic":"Set laws & De Morgan","type":"mcq","q":"Which is De Morgan's second law in the course's set-difference form?","options":["A − (B ∩ C) = (A − B) ∩ (A − C)","A − (B ∪ C) = (A − B) ∪ (A − C)","A − (B ∩ C) = (A − B) ∪ (A − C)","A − (B ∩ C) = (A ∩ B) − C"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"The operation flips: difference over ∩ gives ∪ (slide 53, homework on slide 59). The second option keeps ∩, which is the common slip.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 53, 59","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Set theory laws (slide 53)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-028","chapter":"l03","topic":"Set laws & De Morgan","type":"mcq","q":"Which law is (A ∪ B) ∩ A = A?","options":["Distributivity","Idempotency","Absorption","Associativity"],"answer":2,"fixed":false,"why":"Slide 53: absorption is (A ∪ B) ∩ A = A and (A ∩ B) ∪ A = A. Idempotency is A ∪ A = A.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 53","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Set theory laws (slide 53)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-029","chapter":"l03","topic":"Set laws & De Morgan","type":"tf","q":"Slide 56's numerical check (A = {1,2,3,4}, B = {2,5}, C = {3,6}) proves De Morgan's first law.","options":["True","False"],"answer":1,"fixed":false,"why":"A numerical check confirms one instance only. The proof is the double inclusion L ⊆ R and R ⊆ L on slides 54–55.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 54–56","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Proving De Morgan's first law (slides 54–56)","origin":"practice"},{"id":"l03-030","chapter":"l03","topic":"Set laws & De Morgan","type":"tf","q":"To prove L = R for De Morgan's first law, slides 54–55 show L ⊆ R and R ⊆ L. (Slide 54 prints \"L < R and R < L\", a typo for ⊆.)","options":["True","False"],"answer":0,"fixed":false,"why":"Set equality is proved by double inclusion, the same definition of A = B as Lecture 1 slide 15. \"<\" is a typo for ⊆.","source":"CS313 TComp - Lecture 2 slide 54–55","study":"03-proof-techniques.md — Proving De Morgan's first law (slides 54–56)","origin":"practice"}]};
